2400:
** ἰδοῦ**
(ἴδου Hdn.Gr. cited in {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus “Scholia in Theodosii Canones” 2.140)}}_aorist 2_ _imperative_ _middle_ of ὁρά; but,
**II** **ἰδού** (on the accent see Hdn.Gr.{{Refs|1.417, +others), as}} _adverb_, **lo! behold!** (even with words of hearing, ἰδοὺ δοῦπον αὖ κλύω τινά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 870 (Lyric poetry), compare “El.” 1410):}}
**II.1** with Nouns and Prons., ἰ. χελιδών Klein {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“Meistersign.” 133 (Attic vase, 6th c.BC)}}, etc.; ἰ. ἐγώ **here am** I, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Gen.27.1, +others; ἰ. ἡ μνᾶ σοι NT.Luke.19.20}}; οὐκ ἰ. Ἀαρώ; {{LXX|LXX.Exo.4.14.}}
**II.2** with Verbs,
**II.2.a** in the _imperative_, ἰ. θεᾶσθε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1079, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 366}}; especially in offering a thing, **take it**! ἰ. δέχου παῖ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 776.}}
**II.2.b** in _indicative_ of all tenses, ἰ. πείθομαι {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 143 (Lyric poetry): frequently in LXX and “NT”}} with past tenses, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Gen.24.15, +others, NT.Matt.27.51, +others}}; in the middle of a sentence, {{NT|NT.Luke.13.16.}}
**II.3** with questions, ἰ., τί ἔστι; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 825, “Eq.” 157.}}
**II.4** in repeating another's words quizzingly, as ἰδού γ᾽ ἄκρατον wine, **quotha**! {{Refs|[prev. work] 87}}; ἰ. λέγειν{{Refs|[prev. work] 344; ἰδού γε κλέπτειν [prev. author] “Th.” 206, compare “Ec.” 136.}}

2402:
** ἱδρ-ώς**
(see. near the end), ῶτος, ὁ, and _Aeolic dialect_ ἡ, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 2.13}}; _dative_ ἱδρῶτι, _accusative_ ἱδρῶτ; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus has _dative_ ἱδρῷ (not ἱδρῶ as 4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus “Scholia in Theodosii Canones” 1.248) 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.385, 745}}; _accusative_ ἱδρῶ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|11.621, 22.2, compare 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.87, 4.656}}: (ἶδος):— **sweat**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (see. below), etc.; μετὰ ἱδρῶτος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 350d; κατὰ δ᾽ ἱ. ἔρρεεν ἐκ μελέων 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.599; ἱ. ἀνῄει χρωτί 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 767}}; στάζειν ἱδρῶτι (see. στάζω); ῥέεσθαι ἱδρῶτι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Coriolanus” 3}}; of **sweat** as the sign of toil, τῆς ἀρετῆς ἱδρῶτα θεοὶ προπάροιθεν ἔθηκαν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 289; ἱδρῶτα παρέχειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.29: _plural_, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 4.36, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 867a13, etc.}}; ἱδρῶτες ξηροί, opposed to the effect of baths, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 239c.}}
**2** **exudation** of trees, **gum, resin**, σμύρνης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1175; δρυός Ion Trag. 40}}; Βρομιάδος ἱδρῶτα πηγῆς, of wine, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 52.12.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **anything earned by the sweat of one's brow**, οὐ γὰρ τὸν ἐμὸν ἱδρῶτα.. ἐκβαλῶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 750, cf. Chor. p.270 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus (_plural_). [ῑ in _Epic dialect_ and Lyric poetry: ῐ in E.[same place]] (Cf. ἰδίω.)}}

2406:
** ἱερᾱτ-ευμα**, ατος, τό,
**priesthood**, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Exo.19.6, NT.1Pet.2.9.}}
**2** **body of priests**, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|[prev. work = 6th c.AD: Procopius Caesariensis Historicus “de Aedificiis”] 5.}}

2407:
** ἱερᾱτ-εύω**,
_Ionic dialect_ **ἱερητ-** {{Refs|“GDI” 5394}} (later also in Northern Greece, _Boeotian dialect_, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“IG” 7.3097 (perhaps 1st c.BC); 6th c.BC: Phocylides Lyricus, [prev. work] 9(1).32.40 (Stiris, 2nd c.BC), +others; Thess., [prev. work] 9(2).333 (1st c.AD),compare “SIG” 2588.110 (Delos, 2nd c.BC)}}; Lesb. ἰρητεύω {{Refs|“IG” 12(2).527.45)}}:—**to be priest** or **priestess**, θεῶν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“OGI” 90.51 (Rosetta, 2nd c.BC); Καίσαρος probably [prev. work]767.4 (from Cyrene)}}; τοῦ Διὸς τοῦ Σωτῆρος {{NT+4th c.BC+|“IG” 7.2727 (Acraeph., 1st c.BC); τᾷ Ἀθάνᾳ [prev. work]9(1).65 (from Daulis),compare 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 5.6.3: _absolutely_, “SIG” 1044.19 (Halic., 4th-3rd c.BC), +others, LXX.Exo.28.1, NT.Luke.1.8}}:—also **ἱερᾱτ-εύομαι**, {{Refs|“IGRom.” 4.539 (from Cotiaeum).}}
**II** _passive_, **to be made holy**, Zos.Alch.p.108B.

2409:
** ἱερ-εύς** [ῐ], έως,
_Ionic dialect_ -ῆος, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|5th c.AD: Cypria -ῆϝος “Inscription Cyprian dialect” 59 H., ὁ, _Attic dialect_ _plural_ ἱερῆς: _genitive_ _sing._ and _plural_ written ἱερείως, ἱερείων, “PStrassb.” 83.2, 9 (2nd c.BC)}}:—_Ionic dialect_ _nominative_ **ἱρεύς** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.10, 16.604, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.198}}: _Doric dialect_ **ἱᾰρεύς** {{Refs|“IG” 4.1182, +others (_genitive_ ἰαρέος [prev. work]1580)}}; _accusative_ _plural_ τὸς ἱαρές {{Refs|“Schwyzer” 236 (from Cyrene); _nominative_ _plural_ οἱ ἱαρές [prev. passage]; _nominative_ _sing._ ἱαρές “GDI” 4846 (from Cyrene)}}:— also **ἱέρεως** (_Attic dialect_ and _proparoxytone_ _accusative_ to {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus “Scholia in Theodosii Canones” 1.253) “SIG” 1037.4 (Milet., 4th-3rd c.BC)}}; _genitive_ ἱέρεω {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IPE” 12.32 A 23, +others (Olbia, 3rd c.BC)}}; _dative_ ἵρεῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Schwyzer” 692 (Chios, 5th c.BC)}}; _accusative_ ἱέρεω {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Milet.” 1(7).203b3 (2nd c.BC)}}: **ἱερής** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 5(2).115.1 (Tegea, 4th-3rd c.BC),compare “Inscription Cyprian dialect” 100 H.:— 4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus}} **ἱαρής** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 5(2).13.10 (3rd c.BC): _accusative_ ἱερήν [prev. work]3.1 (Tegea, 4th c.BC)}}: (ἱερός):—**priest, sacrificer, diviner**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.62, 16.604, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.17, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.2, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.124, etc.}}; ἐφ᾽ ἱερέως, as a date, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 332.1 (Potidaea, 4th-3rd c.BC)}}, etc. (frequently unaspirated, ἐπ᾽ ἰερέως {{Refs|“IG” 12(1).890, etc. (from Rhodes))}}; of the Jewish High Priest, {{LXX+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 34 / 5.1; ἱ. ὁ μέγας LXX.Lev.21.10, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.591; ἱ. ὁ χριστός LXX.Lev.4.5}}; at Rome, = **pontifex,** {{Refs|Mon.Anc.Gr. 6.9}}; ἱ. ὁ μέγιστος, = **pont. maximus**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 27.2.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, ἱ. τις ἄτας **a minister** of woe, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 735 (Lyric poetry); comically, λεπτοτάτων λήρων ἱερεῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 359}}; ἱ. Διονύσου, of a winebibber, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 19; ἱ. φιλοσοφίας 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 52.42.}}

2411:
** ἱερός**
(for** ἱερὸν ** see. III.2), ά, όν, also ός, όν in the phrase ἱερὸς ἀκτή {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 597, 805, Oracle texts cited in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.77}}: _Ionic dialect_ and _poetry_ **ἱρός**, ή, όν (see. entry near the end): _Doric dialect_ and N. Greek **ἱᾰρός** {{Refs|“IG” 22.1126.20, etc.}}: _Aeolic dialect_ **ἶρος** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 23.25, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 8.4}}, but **ἴαρος** (_correlated_ from ἴερ-) {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 20a. 6}}: _superlative_ ἱερώτατος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 582 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 755e.}}
**I** **filled with** or **manifesting divine power, supernatural**, ἱ. ἲς Τηλεμάχοιο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.409, +others; ἱ. μένος Ἀλκινόοιο 8.421, etc.}}; ἄλφιτον, ἀλωαί, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.631, 5.499; Δημήτερος ἱερὸς ἀκτή 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Op.” I [prev. cited]}}; of natural objects or phenomena, rivers, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.351, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.726, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 410 (Lyric poetry)}}; λιβάς, of the Spercheus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1215 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἱεραὶ βῆσσαι Κίρκης 'faery', {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.275}}; ἱ. ἦμαρ, κνέφας, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.66, 11.194; φάος 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 339}}; ἱερὸς δίφρος (where δ. perhaps = ἵπποι) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.464; after 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, ἱ. χεῦμα θαλάσσης 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 192 (_anapaest meter_); ἱ. κῦμα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1206, compare “Cyc.” 265; ὄμβρος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1428; δρόσοι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 117 (Lyric poetry)}}; ὕπνος, of death, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 11}}; ἔστι μὲν οὐδὲν ἱ. no **great** matter, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.22.}}
**II** of divine things, **holy**, ἱεροῖς ἐν δώμασι Κίρκης {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.426; ἱ. γένος ἀθανάτων 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 21; λέχος, of Zeus, [prev. work] 57}}; δόσις the gift **of God**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 93}}; πόλεμος **holy** war, '**crusade**', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 556, etc.}}
**II.2** of earthly things, **hallowed, consecrated**, βωμοί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.305}}; ἱ. δόμος, of the temple of Athena,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|6.89; ἱ. ἑκατόμβη 1.99, 431, etc.; ἐλαίη 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.372; χοαί 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 469, etc.}}; ἱρὰ γράμματα **hieroglyphics**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.36}}; but ἱ. γράμματα of the “Holy Scriptures”, {{NT|NT.2Tim.3.15}}; ἱ. βύβλοι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“OGI” 56.70 (Canopus, 3rd c.BC)}}; ἱ. ἄγαλμα, τρίπους, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1379, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 512, etc.; χρήματα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 568d, etc.; ἱ. τὸ σῶμα τῷ θεῷ δίδωμ᾽ ἔχειν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1285}}; ἱ. σώματα, of ἱερόδουλοι, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 6.2.6; χῆνες 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.325c}}; of animals regarded as 'taboo', [κριοί] εἰσί σφι ἱ. διὰ τοῦτο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.42; so perhaps ἱ. ἰχθύς 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.407}}; of the Roman Tribunes,= Latin _sacrosanctus_, ἱ. καὶ ἄσυλος {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Tiberius Gracchus” 15, etc.}}; of Augustus, {{Refs|“Mon.Anc.Gr.” 5.17}}; ἱ. νόμος law **of sacrifice**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.35, compare “SIG” 685.81 (2nd c.BC)}}; ἱ. λόγος **legend**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.81, etc.; οἱ παλαιοὶ καὶ ἱ. λόγοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 335a}}; ἱ. γάμος **mystical** marriage, a religious ceremony, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 320, 9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc.}} see entry ; opposed to βέβηλος, as **sacred** to **profane**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 7.8, “Anecdota Graeca - Bekker” 223; but more frequently ἱ. καὶ ὅσιος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.52, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Vectigalibus” 5.4, etc.}}; compare ὅσιος.
**II.3** **under divine protection**, frequently of places, Ἴλιος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.648, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 8.4; Πύλος 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.108; Θήβη 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 20a. 6}}; Τροίης ἱερὸν πτολίεθρον, Τροίης ἱερὰ κρήδεμνα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.2, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.100; Ἀθῆναι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.323, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 75, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1221 (Lyric poetry), Ps.-Oracle texts cited in 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1037; Σούνιον ἱρόν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.278}}; ἱ. κύκλος the judge's seat **under the protection of Zeus**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.504: with _genitive_ of the divinity, ἄλσος ἱρὸν Ἀθηναίης, ἄντρον ἱρὸν νυμφάων, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.322, 13.104, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.80, 2.41, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 937, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.3.13, etc.; γῆ καὶ ἑστία ἱερὰ πᾶσι πάντων θεῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 955e}}; χωρίον ὡς -ώτατον{{Refs|[prev. work] 755e, compare “Ti.” 45a}}; with _genitive_ of a human being, Γναθίου.. ἱ. εἰμι {{Refs|“IG” 12.920.}}
**II.3.b** of persons, φυλάκων ἱ. τέλος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.56; ἱ. πυλαωροί 24.681; στρατός 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.81; βασιλέες 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 5.97}}; ἱ. εὐσεβής τε, of Oedipus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 287}}; ἅνθρωπος ἱ. **initiated**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 652}}; with _genitive_ of a divinity, **deuoted, dedicated**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 75, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 85b.}}
**II.3.c** under the Roman Empire,= **sacer, imperial**, ἐκ τῶν ἱερῶν τοῦ Καίσαρος γραμμάτων {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“IGRom.” 4.571 (Aezani, 2nd c.AD); ὁ -ώτατος φίσκος, τὸ -ώτατον ταμιεῖον, [prev. work]3.727 (from Lycia), “SIG” 888.10 (Scaptopara, 3rd c.AD)}}, etc.; τὸ -ώτατον βῆμα (of the **praefectus Aegypti**), {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PHamb.” 4.8 (1st c.AD)}}: generally, **worshipful**, ἱ. σύνοδος {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“OGI” 713.9 (Egypt, 3rd c.AD), etc.}}
**III** as _substantive_,
**III.1** **ἱερά**, _Ionic dialect_ **ἱρά**, τά, **offerings, victims**, ἱερὰ ῥέξας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.147, etc.; ἔρδειν 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 336; διδόναι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.184; ἀλλ᾽ ὅ γε δέκτο μὲν ἱρά 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.420, compare 23.207: less frequently in _sing._, ὄφρ᾽ ἱρὸν ἑτοιμασσαίατ᾽ Ἀθήνῃ 10.571; θῦσαι ἱρά 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.59, 8.54, etc.; θυσίας καὶ ἱρὰ ποιέειν [prev. author] 2.63; αἴθειν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1033; ἱ. πατρῷα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 1015; ἱ. ἐπιχώρια 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 259.}}
**III.1.b** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, **omens afforded by sacrifice**, τὰ ἱρὰ οὐ προεχώρεε χρηστά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.44}}; τὰ ἱερὰ καλὰ [ἦν] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.8.15; simply οὐκ ἐγίγνετο τὰ ἱ. [prev. work] 2.2.3.}}
**III.1.c** generally, **sacred objects** or **rites**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.172, 4.33; τῶν ὑμετέρων ἱ. καὶ κοινῶν μετεῖχον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.3}}; of **cult-images, IG Rom.**{{Refs|3.800 (syllium).}}
**III.2** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, **ἱερόν**, _Ionic dialect_ **ἱρόν**, τό, **holy place**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.119, +others}}; opposed to νηός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.170, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.90, 5.18}}; frequently of a **temple**, ἔστι δὲ ἐν τῷ τεμένεϊ.. ἱρόν κτλ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.112}}; of the Jewish temple, {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|LXX.1Ch.29.4, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 16.39.4, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 16.2.34, NT.Matt.24.1.}}
**III.3** ἱερὸν τῆς δίκης **a sacred principle** of right, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1002.}}
**III.4** **ἱερός**, ὁ (i.e. μήν), name of month at Delos, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 12.377.22, 11(2).203 A 31 (3rd c.BC).}}
**III.5** ἱεροί, οἱ, members of a religious college or guild, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. work]5(1).1390.1, +others (Andania, 1st c.BC), probably in “SIG” 1010.7 (from Chalcedon)}}, etc.; also of women, **ἱεραί**, αἱ, {{Refs|“IG” 5(1). [same place], compare 1511 (from Sparta).}}
**III.5.b** ={ἱερόδουλος}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1356 (Messenia, 5th c.BC), “Inscription Perg.” 572, “GDI” 5702.39 (from Samos).}}
**IV** special phrases, post-{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}
**IV.1** proverbial, ἱ. ἄγκυρα **one's last hope**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.815d, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “JTr.” 51, “Fug.” 13, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 1.93, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 11.182.}}
**IV.2** ἱ. βόλος, name of a throw at dice, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 57.1.}}
**IV.3** ἱ. βοτάνη, see at {βοτάνη}.
**IV.4** ἱ. (i.e. γραμμή) {{Refs|compare (γραμμή 111.1)}}, **last line** of draught-board, κινήσαις τὸν ἀπ᾽ ἴρας.. λίθον {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 82, compare 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 225, 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 127}}; τὴν ἀφ᾽ ἱερᾶς (variant{τὴν ἱεράν}) {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Coriolanus” 32.}}
**IV.5** ἱ. ἰχθύς,= ἀνθίας, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 620b35, compare 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 7.282e, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.981d.}}
**IV.6** ἱ. λόχος, see at {λόχος}.
**IV.7** ἱερά (i.e. νίκη), ἡ, **drawn contest, dead heat** (because the prize was assigned to the god), {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“SIG” 1073.48 (6th c.AD: Olympiodorus Philosophus); ποιῆσαι ἱεράν, of the competitor, Wood “Ephesus, App.” vi p.70; so ἱ. ἀθλήματα “Inscription Olymp.” 56; ἱερὸς ὁ στέφανος ἐκρίθη “IG” 9(2).525 (from Larissa); τὸ παγκράτιον ἱ. ἐγένετο [prev. work]527 ([prev. passage]); ἱερός (i.e. ἀγών) [prev. work]7.2727.19, 24 (Acraeph.): _metaphorically_, ἱερὸν ποιῆσαι τὸν στέφανον}} 'divide the honours', {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.58.5, 29.8.9.}}
**IV.8** ἱ. νόσος **epilepsy**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.33, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Morb. Sacr.” at Title Page, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 9.11.3, etc., compare 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Aetia” 3.1.14: _metaphorically_, τὴν οἴησιν ἱ. νόσον ἔλεγε 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 46 (= 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 224).}}
**IV.9** ἡ ἱ. ὁδός **the sacred** road to Delphi, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.34; also, from Athens to Eleusis, 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 61, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 1.36.3, 1st-2nd c.AD: Harpocratio Grammaticus}} see entry ; and that from Elis to Olympia, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 5.25.7.}}
**IV.10** ἱ. ὀστέον, **os sacrum**, the last bone of the spine, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 45, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.981d, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 5.8, etc.}}
**IV.11** ἱ. συμβουλή **sacred duty of an adviser**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 321c, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.6.4, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theages” 122b, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Rh.Pr.” 1.}}
**IV.12** ἱ. σῦριγξ **spinal canal**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.180.}}
**IV.13** ἱερὰ τριήρης, of the Delian ship, or one of the state-ships (Salaminia or Paralos), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.34.}}
**IV.14** frequently in geographical names, e.g. ἱ ἄκρα, in Lycia, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 14.3.8}}; ἱ. ἀκρωτήριον, in Spain, **Cape St. Vincent**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.4.3}}; ἱ. κώμη, in Lydia, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 16.1.8}}; ἱ. νῆσος, one of the Liparean group, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.88}}; one of the **insulae Aegates**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.60.3.}}
**V** _adverb_ -ρῶς **holily**, ἀποθανεῖν variant in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 27. [ῐ by nature, but sometimes ῑ in _Epic dialect_, especially in endings of hexameters, ἱ. ἰχθύς, ἱ. ἦμαρ, ἱερὰ ῥέξας, ἀλφίτου ἱεροῦ ἀκτή, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.407, 8.66, 1.147, 11.631}}; ῑερόν in the first foot of a hexameters, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.22}}; also in compounds ἱεραγωγός, ἱεροθαλλής, ἱερόφωνος: ῑ always in _contraction_ form ἱρός which is used in _Epic dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus}}, and some _Ionic dialect_ _inscriptions_, as {{Refs 7th c.BC+|“IG” 12(8).265.9 (from Thasos), compare 7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus 7.56, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 4.79, +others}}, but is rarely found in codices of {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus (never in 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus or 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus)}}; also in Trag., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 268, etc., but never required by metre in Lyric poetry of Comedy texts]}}

2413:
** ἱερός**
(for** ἱερὸν ** see. III.2), ά, όν, also ός, όν in the phrase ἱερὸς ἀκτή {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 597, 805, Oracle texts cited in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.77}}: _Ionic dialect_ and _poetry_ **ἱρός**, ή, όν (see. entry near the end): _Doric dialect_ and N. Greek **ἱᾰρός** {{Refs|“IG” 22.1126.20, etc.}}: _Aeolic dialect_ **ἶρος** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 23.25, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 8.4}}, but **ἴαρος** (_correlated_ from ἴερ-) {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 20a. 6}}: _superlative_ ἱερώτατος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 582 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 755e.}}
**I** **filled with** or **manifesting divine power, supernatural**, ἱ. ἲς Τηλεμάχοιο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.409, +others; ἱ. μένος Ἀλκινόοιο 8.421, etc.}}; ἄλφιτον, ἀλωαί, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.631, 5.499; Δημήτερος ἱερὸς ἀκτή 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Op.” I [prev. cited]}}; of natural objects or phenomena, rivers, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.351, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.726, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 410 (Lyric poetry)}}; λιβάς, of the Spercheus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1215 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἱεραὶ βῆσσαι Κίρκης 'faery', {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.275}}; ἱ. ἦμαρ, κνέφας, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.66, 11.194; φάος 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 339}}; ἱερὸς δίφρος (where δ. perhaps = ἵπποι) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.464; after 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, ἱ. χεῦμα θαλάσσης 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 192 (_anapaest meter_); ἱ. κῦμα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1206, compare “Cyc.” 265; ὄμβρος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1428; δρόσοι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 117 (Lyric poetry)}}; ὕπνος, of death, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 11}}; ἔστι μὲν οὐδὲν ἱ. no **great** matter, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.22.}}
**II** of divine things, **holy**, ἱεροῖς ἐν δώμασι Κίρκης {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.426; ἱ. γένος ἀθανάτων 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 21; λέχος, of Zeus, [prev. work] 57}}; δόσις the gift **of God**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 93}}; πόλεμος **holy** war, '**crusade**', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 556, etc.}}
**II.2** of earthly things, **hallowed, consecrated**, βωμοί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.305}}; ἱ. δόμος, of the temple of Athena,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|6.89; ἱ. ἑκατόμβη 1.99, 431, etc.; ἐλαίη 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.372; χοαί 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 469, etc.}}; ἱρὰ γράμματα **hieroglyphics**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.36}}; but ἱ. γράμματα of the “Holy Scriptures”, {{NT|NT.2Tim.3.15}}; ἱ. βύβλοι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“OGI” 56.70 (Canopus, 3rd c.BC)}}; ἱ. ἄγαλμα, τρίπους, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1379, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 512, etc.; χρήματα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 568d, etc.; ἱ. τὸ σῶμα τῷ θεῷ δίδωμ᾽ ἔχειν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1285}}; ἱ. σώματα, of ἱερόδουλοι, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 6.2.6; χῆνες 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.325c}}; of animals regarded as 'taboo', [κριοί] εἰσί σφι ἱ. διὰ τοῦτο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.42; so perhaps ἱ. ἰχθύς 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.407}}; of the Roman Tribunes,= Latin _sacrosanctus_, ἱ. καὶ ἄσυλος {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Tiberius Gracchus” 15, etc.}}; of Augustus, {{Refs|“Mon.Anc.Gr.” 5.17}}; ἱ. νόμος law **of sacrifice**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.35, compare “SIG” 685.81 (2nd c.BC)}}; ἱ. λόγος **legend**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.81, etc.; οἱ παλαιοὶ καὶ ἱ. λόγοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 335a}}; ἱ. γάμος **mystical** marriage, a religious ceremony, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 320, 9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc.}} see entry ; opposed to βέβηλος, as **sacred** to **profane**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 7.8, “Anecdota Graeca - Bekker” 223; but more frequently ἱ. καὶ ὅσιος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.52, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Vectigalibus” 5.4, etc.}}; compare ὅσιος.
**II.3** **under divine protection**, frequently of places, Ἴλιος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.648, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 8.4; Πύλος 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.108; Θήβη 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 20a. 6}}; Τροίης ἱερὸν πτολίεθρον, Τροίης ἱερὰ κρήδεμνα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.2, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.100; Ἀθῆναι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.323, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 75, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1221 (Lyric poetry), Ps.-Oracle texts cited in 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1037; Σούνιον ἱρόν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.278}}; ἱ. κύκλος the judge's seat **under the protection of Zeus**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.504: with _genitive_ of the divinity, ἄλσος ἱρὸν Ἀθηναίης, ἄντρον ἱρὸν νυμφάων, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.322, 13.104, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.80, 2.41, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 937, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.3.13, etc.; γῆ καὶ ἑστία ἱερὰ πᾶσι πάντων θεῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 955e}}; χωρίον ὡς -ώτατον{{Refs|[prev. work] 755e, compare “Ti.” 45a}}; with _genitive_ of a human being, Γναθίου.. ἱ. εἰμι {{Refs|“IG” 12.920.}}
**II.3.b** of persons, φυλάκων ἱ. τέλος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.56; ἱ. πυλαωροί 24.681; στρατός 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.81; βασιλέες 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 5.97}}; ἱ. εὐσεβής τε, of Oedipus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 287}}; ἅνθρωπος ἱ. **initiated**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 652}}; with _genitive_ of a divinity, **deuoted, dedicated**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 75, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 85b.}}
**II.3.c** under the Roman Empire,= **sacer, imperial**, ἐκ τῶν ἱερῶν τοῦ Καίσαρος γραμμάτων {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“IGRom.” 4.571 (Aezani, 2nd c.AD); ὁ -ώτατος φίσκος, τὸ -ώτατον ταμιεῖον, [prev. work]3.727 (from Lycia), “SIG” 888.10 (Scaptopara, 3rd c.AD)}}, etc.; τὸ -ώτατον βῆμα (of the **praefectus Aegypti**), {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PHamb.” 4.8 (1st c.AD)}}: generally, **worshipful**, ἱ. σύνοδος {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“OGI” 713.9 (Egypt, 3rd c.AD), etc.}}
**III** as _substantive_,
**III.1** **ἱερά**, _Ionic dialect_ **ἱρά**, τά, **offerings, victims**, ἱερὰ ῥέξας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.147, etc.; ἔρδειν 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 336; διδόναι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.184; ἀλλ᾽ ὅ γε δέκτο μὲν ἱρά 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.420, compare 23.207: less frequently in _sing._, ὄφρ᾽ ἱρὸν ἑτοιμασσαίατ᾽ Ἀθήνῃ 10.571; θῦσαι ἱρά 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.59, 8.54, etc.; θυσίας καὶ ἱρὰ ποιέειν [prev. author] 2.63; αἴθειν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1033; ἱ. πατρῷα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 1015; ἱ. ἐπιχώρια 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 259.}}
**III.1.b** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, **omens afforded by sacrifice**, τὰ ἱρὰ οὐ προεχώρεε χρηστά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.44}}; τὰ ἱερὰ καλὰ [ἦν] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.8.15; simply οὐκ ἐγίγνετο τὰ ἱ. [prev. work] 2.2.3.}}
**III.1.c** generally, **sacred objects** or **rites**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.172, 4.33; τῶν ὑμετέρων ἱ. καὶ κοινῶν μετεῖχον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.3}}; of **cult-images, IG Rom.**{{Refs|3.800 (syllium).}}
**III.2** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, **ἱερόν**, _Ionic dialect_ **ἱρόν**, τό, **holy place**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.119, +others}}; opposed to νηός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.170, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.90, 5.18}}; frequently of a **temple**, ἔστι δὲ ἐν τῷ τεμένεϊ.. ἱρόν κτλ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.112}}; of the Jewish temple, {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|LXX.1Ch.29.4, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 16.39.4, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 16.2.34, NT.Matt.24.1.}}
**III.3** ἱερὸν τῆς δίκης **a sacred principle** of right, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1002.}}
**III.4** **ἱερός**, ὁ (i.e. μήν), name of month at Delos, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 12.377.22, 11(2).203 A 31 (3rd c.BC).}}
**III.5** ἱεροί, οἱ, members of a religious college or guild, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. work]5(1).1390.1, +others (Andania, 1st c.BC), probably in “SIG” 1010.7 (from Chalcedon)}}, etc.; also of women, **ἱεραί**, αἱ, {{Refs|“IG” 5(1). [same place], compare 1511 (from Sparta).}}
**III.5.b** ={ἱερόδουλος}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1356 (Messenia, 5th c.BC), “Inscription Perg.” 572, “GDI” 5702.39 (from Samos).}}
**IV** special phrases, post-{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}
**IV.1** proverbial, ἱ. ἄγκυρα **one's last hope**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.815d, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “JTr.” 51, “Fug.” 13, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 1.93, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 11.182.}}
**IV.2** ἱ. βόλος, name of a throw at dice, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 57.1.}}
**IV.3** ἱ. βοτάνη, see at {βοτάνη}.
**IV.4** ἱ. (i.e. γραμμή) {{Refs|compare (γραμμή 111.1)}}, **last line** of draught-board, κινήσαις τὸν ἀπ᾽ ἴρας.. λίθον {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 82, compare 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 225, 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 127}}; τὴν ἀφ᾽ ἱερᾶς (variant{τὴν ἱεράν}) {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Coriolanus” 32.}}
**IV.5** ἱ. ἰχθύς,= ἀνθίας, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 620b35, compare 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 7.282e, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.981d.}}
**IV.6** ἱ. λόχος, see at {λόχος}.
**IV.7** ἱερά (i.e. νίκη), ἡ, **drawn contest, dead heat** (because the prize was assigned to the god), {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“SIG” 1073.48 (6th c.AD: Olympiodorus Philosophus); ποιῆσαι ἱεράν, of the competitor, Wood “Ephesus, App.” vi p.70; so ἱ. ἀθλήματα “Inscription Olymp.” 56; ἱερὸς ὁ στέφανος ἐκρίθη “IG” 9(2).525 (from Larissa); τὸ παγκράτιον ἱ. ἐγένετο [prev. work]527 ([prev. passage]); ἱερός (i.e. ἀγών) [prev. work]7.2727.19, 24 (Acraeph.): _metaphorically_, ἱερὸν ποιῆσαι τὸν στέφανον}} 'divide the honours', {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.58.5, 29.8.9.}}
**IV.8** ἱ. νόσος **epilepsy**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.33, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Morb. Sacr.” at Title Page, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 9.11.3, etc., compare 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Aetia” 3.1.14: _metaphorically_, τὴν οἴησιν ἱ. νόσον ἔλεγε 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 46 (= 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 224).}}
**IV.9** ἡ ἱ. ὁδός **the sacred** road to Delphi, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.34; also, from Athens to Eleusis, 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 61, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 1.36.3, 1st-2nd c.AD: Harpocratio Grammaticus}} see entry ; and that from Elis to Olympia, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 5.25.7.}}
**IV.10** ἱ. ὀστέον, **os sacrum**, the last bone of the spine, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 45, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.981d, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 5.8, etc.}}
**IV.11** ἱ. συμβουλή **sacred duty of an adviser**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 321c, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.6.4, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theages” 122b, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Rh.Pr.” 1.}}
**IV.12** ἱ. σῦριγξ **spinal canal**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.180.}}
**IV.13** ἱερὰ τριήρης, of the Delian ship, or one of the state-ships (Salaminia or Paralos), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.34.}}
**IV.14** frequently in geographical names, e.g. ἱ ἄκρα, in Lycia, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 14.3.8}}; ἱ. ἀκρωτήριον, in Spain, **Cape St. Vincent**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.4.3}}; ἱ. κώμη, in Lydia, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 16.1.8}}; ἱ. νῆσος, one of the Liparean group, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.88}}; one of the **insulae Aegates**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.60.3.}}
**V** _adverb_ -ρῶς **holily**, ἀποθανεῖν variant in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 27. [ῐ by nature, but sometimes ῑ in _Epic dialect_, especially in endings of hexameters, ἱ. ἰχθύς, ἱ. ἦμαρ, ἱερὰ ῥέξας, ἀλφίτου ἱεροῦ ἀκτή, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.407, 8.66, 1.147, 11.631}}; ῑερόν in the first foot of a hexameters, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.22}}; also in compounds ἱεραγωγός, ἱεροθαλλής, ἱερόφωνος: ῑ always in _contraction_ form ἱρός which is used in _Epic dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus}}, and some _Ionic dialect_ _inscriptions_, as {{Refs 7th c.BC+|“IG” 12(8).265.9 (from Thasos), compare 7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus 7.56, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 4.79, +others}}, but is rarely found in codices of {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus (never in 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus or 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus)}}; also in Trag., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 268, etc., but never required by metre in Lyric poetry of Comedy texts]}}

2416:
** ἱεροσῡλ-έω**,
_perfect_ ἱεροσύληκα {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 417.8 (Delph., 3rd c.BC)}}:—**rob a temple, commit sacrilege**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 845, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.10, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 575b.}}
**II** with _accusative_, ἱ. τὰ ὅπλα **steal the sacred** arms, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.64, compare 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 136}}; ἱ. τὰ ἱερά **rob** or **plunder** the temples, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 30.26.9; θεούς 3rd-2nd c.BC: Phalaris Epistolographus “Epistulae - Epistolographi” 84.1.}}

2417:
** ἱερόσῡλ-ος**, ὁ,
(_proparoxytone_) **temple-robber**, or generally, **sacrilegious person**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 30, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 30.21, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 344b, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 560, etc.: _feminine_, [prev. work] 504: ἱερόσυλε γραῦ [prev. work] 524}}: _neuter_ as _adjective_, ἱ. θηρία {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pk.” 176.}}
**II** of things, **got by sacrilege**, παροψίδες {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 7.4.}}

2418:
** ἱερουργ-έω**,
**perform sacred rites,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|Inscriptiones Graecae 12.4.4, 8, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.94, etc.}}
**II** with _accusative_, ἱ. τὴν κλίνην, Latin _lectisternium facere_, {{Refs|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” (add.)(from Lebanon)}}; ἱ. ζῷα **sacrifice** them, gloss on{σφάξαι}, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Ammonius Grammaticus “περὶ ὁμοίων καὶ διαφόρων λέξεων” p.127}} V.; ἱ. τὸ εὐαγγέλιον **minister** the gospel, {{NT|NT.Rom.15.16}}; τὸν νόμον variant in {{LXX|LXX.4Mac.7.8}}:—_middle_, ἱερουργίας ἱερουργεῖσθαι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 31:}} —_passive_, τὰ ἱερουργηθέντα **victims offered**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 5.5.9, compare 4th c.AD: Palaephatus Paradoxographus 51}}; -ούμεναι τελεταί **celebrated**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de vita Pythagorica” 3.14}}; ἱερουργούμενοι βωμοί **consecrated**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “ad Marcellam - Porphyrii Opuscula” 18.}}

2425:
** ἱκᾰν-ός** [ῐ], ή, όν,
(ἱκνέομαι) **sufficing, becoming, befitting**; prose _adjective_, used two or three times by Trag. (see. below):
**I** of persons, **sufficient, competent to do** a thing, with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.45, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.15, etc.}}; ἱ. τεκμηριῶσαι **sufficient** to prove a point, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.9; -ώτατος [εἰπεῖν] καὶ γνῶναι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.42}}; τίς σοῦ -ώτερος πεῖσα; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.12}}; ἱ. ζημιοῦν **with sufficient power** to punish, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Lac.” 8.4; ἱ. βοηθεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 277a, compare “R.” 365a; ἱ. ὥστε γνῶναι [prev. author] “Lg.” 875a, compare “Phdr.” 258b; ἱ. κατὰ τὴν ἐπιφάνειαν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 25.3.6, +others:}} with _accusative_ _of things_, ἀνὴρ γνώμην ἱ. a man of **sufficient** prudence, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.4}}; ἱ. τὴν ἰατρικήν **sufficiently versed in** medicine, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.15: with _dative_ _of things_, ἱ. ἐμπειρίᾳ καὶ ἡλικίᾳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 467d; οἱ τοῖς χρήμασιν -ώτατοι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 2.1}}: with _dative_ _person_, **a match for, equivalent to**, εἷς ἔχων ἰατρικὴν πολλοῖς ἱ. ἰδιώταις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 322c, compare “Tht.” 169a: _absolutely_, ἱ. Ἁπόλλων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 377; οἱ -ώτατοι τῶν πολιτῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.132; κριτὴς -ώτερος [prev. author] 10.38; ἱ. σοφιστής 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 204a}}; αὐληταὶ ἱ. ὡς πρὸς ἰδιώτας **very tolerable** in comparison with.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Prt.” 327c; γυνὴ ἱ. μέν, ἄγροικος δέ 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 20.3}}; ὁ Ἱ. **the Almighty**, {{LXX|LXX.Ruth.1.21.}}
**I.2** in bad sense, **capable**, ἱ. εἶ λαλῶν κατακόψαι πάντα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 69.}}
**II** of things, in amount, **sufficient, adequate**, τὰ ἀρκοῦνθ᾽ ἱ. τοῖς γε σώφροσιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 554; ἱ. τὰ κακὰ καὶ τὰ παρακείμενα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1047}}; ἱκανὰ τοῖς πολεμίοις ηὐτύχηται they have had successes **enough**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.77}}; ἱ. εἴς, ἐπί, πρός τι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 4.9, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 371e, “Prt.” 322b; [πρόβατα] ἱ. ἐς φορβήν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.121}}; of size, **large enough**, οὐχ ἱκανῆς οὔσης τῆς Ἁττικῆς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.2}}; οὐδ᾽ ἦν ἱκανά σοι.. μέλαθρα.. ἐγκαθυβρίζειν not **large enough** to riot in, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 996; χώρα ἱ. τρέφειν τοὺς τότε 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 373d, +others}}; of number or magnitude, **considerable**, λῦπαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.2; μέρος τῶν ὄντων [prev. work] 2.1.6}}, etc.; of Time, **considerable, long**, ἱ. χρόνον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 354 (Lyric poetry); ἱ. χρόνος τινὶ ἐπιλαθέσθαι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.10; ἱκανόν ἐστί τινι 4th-3rd c.BC: Damoxenus Comicus 1.1}}: with personal _construction_, ἔφη ἱκανὸς αὐτὸς ἀτυχῶν εἶναι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 2.7.}}
**II.2** **sufficient, satisfactory**, ἱ. μαρτυρίαν παρέχεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 179b; ἱ. λόγῳ ἀποδεῖξαι [prev. author] “Hp.Mi.” 369c}}; τὸ ἱ. λαμβάνειν to take **security** or **bail,** {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Acts.17.9, “OGI” 629.100 (Palmyra, 2nd c.AD)}}; τὸ ἱ. ποιεῖν give **security**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 32.3.13, 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 4.50, 6th c.AD: Justinianus Imperator “Novellae” 86.4}} (but simply, **satisfy**, τῷ ὄχλῳ {{NT+3rd c.BC+|NT.Mark.15.15); ἱ. δοῦναι “PSI” 6.554.23 (3rd c.BC), “POxy.” 294.23 (1st c.AD)}}; ἐφ᾽ ἱκανόν,= ἱκανῶς, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 11.25.1, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 11.40.}}
**III** _adverb_ -νῶς **sufficiently, adequately**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.92, etc.}}; λαγόνες λαπαραὶ ἱ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 5.30, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physiognomonica” 807b26; ἱ. εἴρηται περί τινος [prev. author] “EN” 1096a3, +others}}; later, **considerably, amply**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 3.6, “VS” 1.8.3, 2nd c.AD(?): Antoninus Liberalis Mythographus 7.7}}; **fully**, μιᾶς ὥρας ἱ. παρελθούσης {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Ptolemaeus Epigrammaticus “Alm.” 4.6.}}
**III.b** **excessively**, οὔτε γὰρ ἱ. ὑγρόν ἐστι not **too** moist, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.765, compare 767,768; ἱ. βλαβερά [prev. author] Vict. _Attic dialect_ 8; παχὺ ἱ. αἷμα [prev. passage]}}
**III.2** ἱ. ἔχειν to be **sufficient**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.91, etc.}}; ἱ. ἐχέτω let this be **enough**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 245e; ἱ. ἔχει πρός τι [prev. author] “R.” 430c, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.3.22; περί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 402a}}; ἱ. ἔχειν τινί to be **sufficiently** supplied **with.. **, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Grg.” 493c; ἱ. ἔχειν τοῦ βάθους [prev. author] “Tht.” 194d; ἐπιστήμης [prev. author] “Phlb.” 62a; ἱ. πεφυκέναι πρὸς τἆλλα [prev. author] “Chrm.” 158b: _absolutely_, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.1.1}}: _superlative_ **-ωτάτως**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τέχνης” 12}}; **-ώτατα** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 67a.}}

2426:
** ἱκᾰν-ότης**, ητος, ἡ,
**sufficiency, fitness**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author = 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus] “Ly.” 215a.}}
**II** **a sufficiency**, παίδων {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 930c.}}

2427:
** ἱκᾰν-όω**,
**make sufficient, qualify**, {{NT|NT.2Cor.3.6}}:—_passive_, **to be empowered,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|PTeb. 20.8 (2nd c.BC)}}; **to be made complete, brought to perfection**, of the soul, τῷ περιέχοντι {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Hierocles Facetiarum Scriptor p.9A.}}
**II** _passive_, **to be satisfied, content**, τινι {{LXX+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 2.74: _absolutely_, Teles p.39 H., cf. LXX.Mal.3.10}}; ἱκανούσθω ὑμῖν, with _infinitive_, **let it suffice** you.., i.e. do it no more, {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.12.28, +others: _absolutely_, LXX.Num.16.7.}}

2428:
** ἱκετ-ήριος**,
_poetry_ **ἱκτήριος**, α, ον, as _adjective_ in the latter form only, **of** or **fit for suppliants**, ἱ. θησαυρός, of hair offered to a god, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1175; κλάδοι [prev. author] “OT” 3; ἱκτήριοι,= ἱκέται, [prev. work] 327}}; φωτῶν ἱκτήρια, ={φῶτας ἱκτηρίους}, {{Refs|[prev. author] “OC” 923.}}
**II** ἱκετηρία, _poetry_ ἱκτηρία, _Ionic dialect_ ἱκετηρίη (i.e. ῥάβδος), ἡ, **olive-branch which the suppliant held in his hand** as a symbol of his condition, λευκοστεφεῖς ἱκτηρίας {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 192}}; ἱκετηρίην λαβεῖν, φέρειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.51, 7.141; ἱκετηρίαν ἔχειν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 383; καταθεῖναι ἐν τῷ Ἐλευσινίῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.110, compare “UPZ” 1.9 (4th c.BC)}}; especially of petitions laid before the Athenian people, ἱ. θεῖναι And.[same place], {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 43.6 (less correctly θέσθαι 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 8.96}}, which is a later use,{{Refs|compare “SIG” 2666.6 (from Samos))}}; ὑπὲρ θυγατρὸς ἱ. τιθεμένη {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PTeb.” 326.3 (3rd c.AD)}}; ἱ. ἔθηκεν παρ᾽ ὑμῖν,= ἱκέτευσεν ὑμᾶς, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.107, compare 24.12; ὑπὲρ τοῦ μισθοῦ ἱ. θεῖναι εἰς τὴν βουλήν 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.104, compare 2.15}}; later ἱ. πέμπειν, προβάλλεσθαι, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 28, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 3.26}}; ἱκετηρίας προσενέγκας, ἱκετηρίαν προσάγειν, {{NT+4th c.AD+|NT.Heb.5.7, “POxy.” 71 i 3 (4th c.AD): _metaphorically_, ἱκετηρίαν δὲ γόνασιν ἐξάπτω σέθεν τὸ σῶμα τοὐμόν}}, where the suppliant represents herself as the olive-branch, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1216; νομίζετε τὸν παῖδα τουτονὶ ἱκετηρίαν προκεῖσθαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 43.83.}}
**II.2** ={ἱκεσία}, variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.138 (_plural_), compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.112.8 (_plural_), 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator}}**ad Ath.**275c, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 7.7.}}

2431:
** ἱλᾰρός** [ῐ], ά, όν,
(ἵλαος) **cheerful, merry**, φέγγος {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 456 (Lyric poetry); ἱλαροὶ ἀντὶ σκυθρωπῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.7.12; ἱλαρὸς ἴσθι 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 17.9, ἱλαρὸν βλέψαι “Anthologia Graeca” 12.159 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus), compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Mus.” p.85 K., 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Philostrati majoris imagines” 1.16; ἱ. δότης NT.2Cor.9.7, cf. LXX.Pro.22.8: in later Greek,= ἵλεως}}, **gracious,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|PMag.Leid.W. 14.12, etc.: τὸ ἱ.,= ἱλαρότης, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Sulla” 34, 4th c.BC: Heraclides Ponticus Historicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 14.624d}}; -ωτέρα ἀγγελία **more cheerful** news, {{Refs|“Jahresh.” 23 “Beibl.” 283 (from Ephesus)}}. _adverb_ **-ρῶς**{{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Apologia Socratis” 33, LXX.Job.22.26, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Mus.” [same place], 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Agesilaus” 2.}}
**II** of blood, **quick-pulsing**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “de gymnastica” 48 (_comparative_).}}
**III** of imitation gold, **bright,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|PLeid.X. 17 (3rd-4th c.AD).}} _adverb_ ἱλαρῶς (to be read ἱλαρῷ){{Refs|[prev. work]87.}}

2433:
** ἱλᾰσ-κομαι**,
_future_ ἱλάσομαι [ᾰ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 95a, _Epic dialect_ ἱλάσσομαι}} Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 8.42.6}} also ἱλάξομαι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.808:}}_aorist 1_ ἱλᾰσάμην, _Epic dialect_ _participle_ ἱλασσάμενοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.100}}, _Epic dialect_ _subjunctive_ _2nd pers._ _singular_ ἱλάσσεαι {{Refs|1.147}}, **-ηαι** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.1037}}; _infinitive_ ἱλάσσασθαι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|2nd c.AD(?): Antoninus Liberalis Mythographus 25.2 codices; also ἱλάξασθαι 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.1093:}} —_passive_ (see. below {{Refs|11)}}. [ῑ regularly (written ι, not ει, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 1044.6,9 (Halic., 4th-3rd c.BC))}}; ῐ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.100, 147]}}: (ἵλαος):—**appease**, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus always of gods, θεὸν ἱ. [prev. work] 386, compare 100, +others, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.419; μολπῇ θεὸν ἱλάσκοντο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.472}}; σπονδῇσι θύεσσί τε ἱλάσκεσθαι (i.e. θεούς) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 338; ὄφρ᾽ ἡμῖν ἑκάεργον ἱλάσσεαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.147}}; with _participle_, ἱλάσκομαι πέμπων by presenting, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.9}}; τοῦτον (i.e. θεὸν) ἱλάσκου ποῶν μηδὲν ἄτοπον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 558}}; of the dead as heroized, θυσίῃσί τινα ἱ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.47.}}
**2** of men, **conciliate**, ἱ. τινὰ χρήμασι {{Refs|[prev. author] 8.112}}; πῶς ἱλασόμεθα καὶ τίνι λόγ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phd.” [same place]; ἱ. τὴν ὀργήν τινος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Mi.” 61.}}
**3** **expiate**, τὰς ἁμαρτίας {{NT|NT.Heb.2.17.}}
**II** _passive_ with _future_ ἱλάσομαι, also ἱλασθής ομαι variant in {{LXX|LXX.4Ki.5.18:}}_aorist 1_ ἱλάσθην{{LXX|LXX.Exo.32.14, +others}}:—**to be merciful, gracious**, τινι [same places]; ἱλάσθητί μοι τῷ ἁμαρτωλῷ {{LXX+NT|NT.Luke.18.13; ταῖς ἁμαρτίαις τινῶν LXX.Psa.77(78).38: with}}_infinitive_, ἱλάσθη κύριος περιποιῆσαι τὸν λαόν{{LXX|LXX.Exo.32.14.}}

2434:
** ἱλᾰσ-μός** [ῑ], ὁ,
**a means of appeasing**, in _plural_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Solon” 12, 5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Argonautica” 39, 554,etc.}}
**2** **atonement, sinoffering**, {{LXX+NT+1st c.AD+|LXX.Eze.44.27, LXX.2Mac.3.33, NT.1John.2.2, 4.10, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.121.}}

2435:
** ἱλᾰσ-τήριος**, α, ον
(ος, ον {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PFay.” 337 (2nd c.AD))}}, **propitiatory, offered in propitiation**, μνῆμα {{LXX+1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 16.7.1; θάνατος LXX.4Mac.17.22}}; θυσίαι {{Refs|“PFay.” [prev. cited]}}
**II** **ἱλαστήριον** ἐπίθεμα, **the mercy-seat**, covering of the ark in the Holy of Holies, {{LXX|LXX.Exo.25.16(17): ἱλαστήρ ¯ ιον}} alone as _substantive_,{{LXX+NT+1st c.AD+|LXX.Lev.16.2, +others, NT.Heb.9.5, compare 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.150.}}
**II.2** (i.e. ἀνάθημα) **propitiatory gift** or **offering,** {{NT|NT.Rom.3.25}}; of a monument, {{Refs|“Inscription Cos” 81,347.}}
**II.3** **monastery**, Men.Prot.p.15 {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}

2436:
Included with: ** ἵλαος**
[see. entry near the end], ον, _Epic dialect_ and Lyric poetry (incl. Lyric poetry of Trag., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 1040, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1480, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 1148)}}: irregular _genitive_ ἱλάονος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“UPZ” 1.8 (4th c.BC)}}; _Attic dialect_ and later **ἵλεως**, ων (also in {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 4.25}}, variant for{ἵλεον} in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.91), dual ἵλεω 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 273e; _nominative_ _plural_ ἵλεῳ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 44, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.1.9 (later ἵλεως}} _indeclinable_ as _nominative_ _plural_, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 985.47 (Philadelphia, 2nd-1st c.BC)}}, as _accusative_ _singular_, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|LXX.2Mac.7.37, 10.26, as _genitive_ _sing._, [prev. work] 2.22); _neuter_ ἵλεα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 95a}}: **ἵλεος**, ον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.94, 6.91 (see. above); also Cretan dialect, “SIG” 527.92 (Dreros, 3rd c.BC), “GDI” 5039.26 (from Hierapytna), 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}: **ἵληϝος**, uncertain in {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“IG” 5(1).1562 (6th c.AD: Olympiodorus Philosophus, 6th-5th c.BC}},= epigram cited in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 5.24.3, where ἱλάῳ)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ **ἴλλαος** {{Refs|Hdn.Gr. 2.524, compare ἱλάεις}}:—of gods, **propitious, gracious**, ἔπειθ᾽ ἵλαος Ὀλύμπιος ἔσσεται ἡμῖν {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.583, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 340, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 782, 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 75, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 3.34, Trag.et 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus [same places], 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.18: in Prose, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 712b, LXX.Gen.43.23, +others, “UPZ” 78.24 (εἵλ-, 2nd c.BC), NT.Heb.8.12, etc.}}; in deprecation, ἵλεώς σοι, κύριε (i.e. ὁ θεός), i.e. be it far from thee, {{NT+4th c.AD+|NT.Matt.16.22; ἵ. ἡμῖν Πλάτων καὶ ἐνταῦθα “OGI” 721.10 (Egypt, 4th c.AD).}}
**2** of things, **propitious, blameless, atoned for**, ἵλαον ἦναι, opposed to ἰνμενφὲς ἦναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 5(2).262(Mantinea, 5th c.BC).}}
**II** of men, **gracious, kindly**, σὺ δ᾽ ἵλαον ἔνθεο θυμόν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.639; σοι.. θυμὸς ἐνὶ φρεσὶν ἵ. ἔστω 19.178; ἵλεως κλύειν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 655; δέξασθαι [prev. author] “Aj.” 1009, compare “Tr.” 763; ἐποίησέ θ᾽ ἱλαρὸν.. κἀπέδειξεν ἵλεων 4th c.BC: Ephippus Comicus 6.7: sometimes almost,= ἱλαρός, μειδῆσαι γελάσαι τε καὶ ἵλαον σχεῖν θυμόν “hymnus ad Cererem” 204, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 206d; ὁ οἶνος τὸν ἄνθρωπον ποιεῖ ἵλεων [prev. author] “Lg.” 649a.}}
**III** _adverb_ ἱλάως and ἱλέως, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus [ῑ always: ᾱ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.583, “hymnus ad Cererem” 204, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus and 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus [same places], 3rd c.BC: Euphorio Epicus 12, “Paean Erythraeus [4th c.BC]” 19, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.18, epigram cited in 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta [same place], “IG” 12(2).476, 1st c.BC: Parthenius Mythographus “fragment” 4}}; elsewhere ᾰ, see above, also {{Refs|[prev. author] “fragment” 32, etc.]}}

2438:
** ἱμάς**, ὁ,
_genitive_ ἱμάντος (not ἱμᾶς, ἱμᾶντος Hdn.Gr. 2.939): _Epic dialect_ _dative_ _plural_ ἱμάντεσσι:— **leathern strap** or **thong**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.262, etc.; ἱμάντα βοός 3.375; βοέους ἱμάντας 22.397: mostly in _plural_, in various senses:}}
**a** **traces**, {{Refs|10.475, 499, +others}}
**b** **reins**, {{Refs|23.324}}, etc.; τμητοῖς ἱμᾶσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 747, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1222.}}
**c** **straps** on which the body of the chariot was hung, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.727.}}
**d** **lash** of a whip, formed by several thongs, {{Refs|23.363.}}
**e** **boxing-glove**, consisting of **several straps put round the hand**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 684, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 6.35, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 342c; ἱ. πυκτικοί 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 22 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}
**2** in _singular_, the magic **girdle** of Aphrodite, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.214, 219.}}
**2.b** **chin-strap** of the helmet, {{Refs|3.371.}}
**2.c** **thong**, by which the bolt was shot home into the socket, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.442, compare 4.802, 21.46.}}
**2.d** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, **thong** or **latchet** of a sandal, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.5.14, 4th c.BC: Ephippus Comicus 14.9, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 109.2, NT.Mark.1.7.}}
**2.e** **rail-rope**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Aristagoras Comicus 5.}}
**2.f** **well-rope**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 10.31, 2nd c.AD: Moeris Grammaticus}}
**2.g** **dog-leash**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 7.6: proverbial, ἦσθ᾽ ἱμὰς κύνειος}} you were tough as a dog-**leash**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 231; also σὺν τῷ κυνὶ καὶ τὸν ἱμάντα 9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc.}}
**2.h** **whip, scourge**, ἔξω τις δότω ἱμάντα {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 74.8, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 106; ἡ διὰ τῶν ἱ. αἰκεία “POxy.” 1186.2 (4th c.AD),cf. NT.Act.22.25; ἱμάντες παιδαγωγῶν 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Epistulae” 911.2.}}
**i** **cord**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 10.1001, compare 1.616.}}
**II** **diseased condition of the uvula**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 17(1).379.}}
**III** ἱμάντες, in Architecture texts, **planks** laid on rafters, {{Refs|“IG” 12.372.82, 373.236, +others, 22.1668.55, 1672.305}}; on στρωτῆρες (which see), {{Refs|[prev. work]463.66. (Cf.Sanskrit}}**sināti** 'bind', Latin _saeta._) [?~X ¯, usually; but also ῑ in _Epic dialect_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.544, etc.: in derivations and compounds always ῐ.]}}

2440:
** ἱμάτιον** [ῑμᾰ-], τό,
in form a _diminutive_ of ἷμα (i.e. εἷμα), **a piece of dress**; in usage always of **an outer garment**, formed by an oblong piece of cloth worn above the χιτών, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 333, “IG” 22.1524.205, +others, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.114, etc.; λαμπρὸν ἱ. ἔχων 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus [277]}}; θοἰμάτιον by crasis for τὸ ἱμ-, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 179, +others; θοἰμάτιον καθεὶς ἄχρι τῶν σφυρῶν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.314; ἱματίων ἕλξεις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.122c}}; of the Roman **toga**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Brutus” 17, “Cor.” 14: hence ἐν ἱματίοις}}, of civilians,= **togati**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cam.” 10}}; but ἱ. Ἑλληνικόν, opposed to the toga, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Merc.Cond.” 25.}}
**2** ἱμάτια, τά, generally, **clothes**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.9, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 279e, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.10; by crasis, θαἰμάτια 6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus 83.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 408 (Lyric poetry), “Lys.” 1093}}; of **grave-clothes**, ἐν εἱμ. τρισὶ [θάπτειν] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12(5).593.2 (Iulis, see/iv 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus), compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Solon” 21.}}
**3** _metaphorically_, ἱμάτια πόλεως τείχη {{Refs|12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1871.50.}}
**II** generally, **cloth**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.23, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 14.109, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 8.7, 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de vita Pythagorica” 21.100. [ἱμ-}} in _Attic dialect_ Inscrr., [{{Refs 1st c.BC+|“IG” 12.427, 386.18, 22.1514.16, etc.; εἱμ- [prev. work]12(5) [prev. cited] (εμ- lap.), 5(1).1390.16, +others(Andania, 1st c.BC), which is easier to explain, see at {εἷμα},{εἱματισμός}.]}}]

2442:
** ἱμείρω** [ῑ],
_Aeolic dialect_ **ἰμέρρω** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 1.27, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 26.5}}, cf. Hdn.Gr. 2.949: (ἵμερος):— **long for, desire**, with _genitive_, τί κακῶν ἱμείρετε τούτων..; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.431, compare 555, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 31; μεγάλων 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 1.62; μάχης 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 940; βίου 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 952, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 435}}: with _infinitive_, **long** or **wish** to do, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus [same place], 6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 13.7, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 233, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 587, “Ichn.” 128; ὄσσα μοι τέλεσσαι θῦμος ἰμέρρει 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica}} [same place]: with _adjective_ _neuter_, γνωτὰ κοὐκ ἄγνωτά μοι προσήλθεθ᾽ ἱμείροντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 59: _absolutely_, [prev. author] “El.” 1053; ἀσμένοις.. καὶ ἱμείρουσιν.. τὸ φῶς ἐγίγνετο 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 418d.}}
**II** _middle_ **ἱμείρομαι**, _aorist 1_ ἱμειράμην _lines_{{Refs|14.163}}:—_passive_, _aorist 1_ ἱμέρθην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.44: with _genitive_, ὁππότ᾽ ἂν ἡβήσῃ τε καὶ ἧς ἱμείρεται αἴης (_Epic dialect_ _aorist_ _subjunctive_) 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.41; χρημάτων ἱ. μεγάλως 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.123: with}}_infinitive_, εἴ πως ἱμείραιτο παραδραθέειν φιλότητι (compare ἵμερος) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.163, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.59, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.120, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 386}}.—_Epic dialect_, _Ionic dialect_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus and 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 4.39)}}, and Trag. word: never in _Attic dialect_ Prose; introduced as _etymology_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cra.” [same place]}}

2443:
** ἵνα**,
_adverb_,
**I** of Place,
**I.1** **in that place, there**, once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, ἵ. γάρ σφιν ἐπέφραδον ἠγερέθεσθαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.127 (_accusative_ to 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis).}}
**I.2** elsewhere _relative_, **in which place, where**,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|2.558, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.136, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.133, 9.27, 54, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.95, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 10.79, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 21, +others, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 22, 855, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1231, etc.}}: rarely in _Attic dialect_ Prose, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.72 (see. below), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 17c, “Phlb.” 61b}}; ἵ. ἡ Νίκη (i.e. ἐστίν) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“IG” 22.1407.13: rare in later Greek, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 1.3.2, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Cont.” 22, “Ind.” 3: with particles, ἵ. τε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.478; ἵ. περ 24.382, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.364, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator}} [same place]; ἵν᾽ ἄν with _subjunctive_, **wherever**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 405, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 315}}; as indirect _interrogative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.179, 2.150, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1008.}}
**I.2.b** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, like other Advs. of Place, with _genitive_, ἵ. τῆς χώρης 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.98}}; ἔμαθε ἵ. ἦν κακοῦ **in what** a calamity, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.213; οὐδ᾽ ὁρᾶν ἵν᾽ ἐ̄ κακοῦ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 367}}; ἵν᾽ ἕσταμεν χρείας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1442; ἵν᾽ ἦμεν ἄτης [prev. author] “El.” 936; ὁρᾷς ἵν᾽ ἐσμὲν αὐτοῦ πέρι τῆς ἀπορίας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 243b.}}
**I.2.c** with Verbs of motion, **whither**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.821, +others; ὁρᾷς ἵν᾽ ἥκεις 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 687, +others, 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 2.10; ιναπερ ὥρμητο 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.74.}}
**II** of circumstance, γάμος.., ἵ. χρή **at which, when**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.27}}; ἵ. μὲν ἐξῆν αὐτοῖς.., ἐνταῦθα.. **when** it was in their power, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.9.}}
**II.2** ={ἐάν}, uncertain in [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.353 (variant{ἵν᾽ ἄν}, cf. Scholia), 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 74.7 codices, variant in 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.1, and 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 176b.}}]
**B** Final _conjunction_, **that, in order that**, from {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} downwards, mostly first word in the clause, but sometimes preceded by an emphatic word, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 169d; ἵ. δή 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.26, 23.207, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.29, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 420e, 610c: never with ἄν}} or κε (if found, these particles belong to the Verb, as in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.156, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1579).}}
**B.I** general usage:
**B.I.1** with _subjunctive_,
**B.I.1.a** after primary tenses of _indicative_, also _subjunctive_ and _imperative_: _present_ _indicative_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.252, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.111, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.2.3, “Cyr.” 1.2.11, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.2}}: _perfect_ _indicative_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.203, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.129}}: _future_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.307, 4.591, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.15}}; _subjunctive_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 364; _imperative_, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.348, +others, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 61, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 880, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 297, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 341b, “Men.” 71d.}}
**B.I.1.b** after historical tenses, in similes, where the _aorist_ is gnomic, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.490 (αὔοι codices)}}; where _aorist_ is treated as equivalent to _perfect_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.99, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.580, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.91, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.4, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.26}}: when the purpose is regarded from the point of view of the speaker's present, σὲ παῖδα ποιεύμην ἵ. μοι.. λοιγὸν ἀμύνῃς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.495, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.29, 6.100, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.44, +others, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.11,12, +others}}
**B.I.1.c** after _optative_ and ἄν, when _optative_ with οὐκ ἄν is used with sense of _imperative_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.264, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.58}}; after βουλοίμην ἄν.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.12.}}
**B.I.1.d** after _imperfect_ with ἄν, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.7.}}
**B.I.2** with _optative_,
**B.I.2.a** after historical tenses, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.3, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.2, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 215, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.11, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 314c, etc.}}: after the historical _present_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 11}}: sometimes both moods, _subjunctive_ and _optative_, follow in consecutive clauses, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.77, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.76, 9.51, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.93, 49.14.}}
**B.I.2.b** after _optative_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.369, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 325; βούλοιντ᾽ ἂν ἡμᾶς ἐξολωλέναι, ἵνα.. λάβοιεν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 413.}}
**B.I.2.c** rarely after primary tenses, by a shifting of the point of view, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.250, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 24, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 410c.}}
**B.I.3** with past tenses of _indicative_,
**B.I.3.a** after unfulfilled wishes, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cri.” 44d.}}
**B.I.3.b** after _indicative_ with ἄν, to express a consequence which has not followed or cannot follow, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1389, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 89b, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 29.17: especially after ἐβουλόμην ἄν.., 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 961, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 4.3.}}
**B.I.3.c** after such Verbs as ἐχρῆν, ἔδει, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 647, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 335c, “Smp.” 181e, “Euthd.” 304e, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.5, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.48, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 349.5, etc.: whenan unfulfilled obligation is implied, τεθαύμακα ὅτι οὐκ εἶπεν (={ἔδει εἰπεῖν}).. ἵ... 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 161c}}]; ἀντὶ τοῦ κοσμεῖν (={δέον κοσμεῖν}).. ἵ... {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 36.47.}}
**B.I.3.d** after _present_ _indicative_ in general statements (including the past), οὐδὲ γὰρ τὸ εἶναι ἔχει ἡ ὕλη, ἱ. ἀγαθοῦ ταύτῃ μετεῖχεν {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 1.8.5.}}
**B.I.4** ἵ. μή as the _negative_ of ἵνα, **that not**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.348, etc.}}
**B.II** special usages:
**B.II.1** like{ὅπως}, after Verbs of command and entreaty, is common only in later Gr. (but[{{NT+8th c.BC+|compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.327 with [prev. work] 19), ἀξιοῦν ἵ... Decrees cited in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.155; δεήσεσθαι ἵ... 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.83; παρακαλεῖν ἵ... 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 3.23.27: frequently in “NT”, ἐκήρυξαν ἵ. μετανοήσωσιν NT.Mark.6.12, +others; of will, ὅσα ἐὰν θέλητε ἵ. ποιῶσιν.. [prev. work] 25: hence ἵ. with _subjunctive_ stands for infinitive, ἐν τούτῳ ἵ. καρπὸν φέρητε (={ἐν τῷ φέρειν}) NT.John.15.8, etc.; πρῶτόν ἐστιν ἵ. κοιμηθῶ 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.10.8, compare 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 8.29}}]; also for ὥστε, {{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.Gen.22.14, +others, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.333a, 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 2.33, etc.}}
**B.II.2** **because**, ἵ. ἀναγνῶ ἐτιμήθην I was honoured **because** I read, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Anonymus cited in 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 266.5, compare “Conj.” 243.21, 4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus “Scholia in Theodosii Canones” 2.257, +others}}; not found in literature.
**B.II.3** elliptical usages,
**B.II.3.a** where the purpose of the utterance is stated, Ζεὺς ἔσθ᾽, ἵν᾽ εἰδῇς 'tis Zeus,— [I tell thee this] **that** thou may'st know it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 989; ἵ. μὴ εἴπω ὅτι οὐδεμιᾷ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 507d; ἵ. συντέμω 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 45.5; ἵν᾽ ἐκ τούτων ἄρξωμαι [prev. author] 21.43}}; ἵ. δῶμεν.. **granted that.. **, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 2.34, compare 1.79.}}
**B.II.3.b** in commands, introducing a principal sentence, ἵ. συντάξῃς **order** him.., {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 240.12 (3rd c.BC)}}; ἵ. λαλήσῃς {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PSI” 4.412.1 (3rd c.BC)}}; ἵ. ἐλθὼν ἐπιθῇς τὰς χεῖρας αὐτῇ {{LXX+NT+5th c.BC+|NT.Mark.5.23,cf. NT.2Cor.8.7, LXX.2Mac.1.9, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 4.1.41, 1st c.BC: Didymus Grammaticus cited in Scholia 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 156.}}
**B.II.3.c** ἵ. τί (i.e. γένηται); **to what end?** either _absolutely_ or as a question, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 719}}; or with a Verb following, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pax” 409, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 26d, etc.}}; ἵ. δὴ τ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1192.}}
**B.II.3.d** in indignant exclamations, **to think that.. !** Σωκράτης ἵ. πάθῃ ταῦτα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.29.16.}}
**B.III** in later Gr. with _indicative_, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Exo.1.11, +others, NT.Gal.2.4, 4.17, etc.}}

2444:
Included with: ** ἵνα**,
_adverb_,
**I** of Place,
**I.1** **in that place, there**, once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, ἵ. γάρ σφιν ἐπέφραδον ἠγερέθεσθαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.127 (_accusative_ to 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis).}}
**I.2** elsewhere _relative_, **in which place, where**,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|2.558, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.136, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.133, 9.27, 54, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.95, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 10.79, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 21, +others, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 22, 855, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1231, etc.}}: rarely in _Attic dialect_ Prose, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.72 (see. below), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 17c, “Phlb.” 61b}}; ἵ. ἡ Νίκη (i.e. ἐστίν) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“IG” 22.1407.13: rare in later Greek, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 1.3.2, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Cont.” 22, “Ind.” 3: with particles, ἵ. τε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.478; ἵ. περ 24.382, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.364, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator}} [same place]; ἵν᾽ ἄν with _subjunctive_, **wherever**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 405, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 315}}; as indirect _interrogative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.179, 2.150, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1008.}}
**I.2.b** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, like other Advs. of Place, with _genitive_, ἵ. τῆς χώρης 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.98}}; ἔμαθε ἵ. ἦν κακοῦ **in what** a calamity, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.213; οὐδ᾽ ὁρᾶν ἵν᾽ ἐ̄ κακοῦ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 367}}; ἵν᾽ ἕσταμεν χρείας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1442; ἵν᾽ ἦμεν ἄτης [prev. author] “El.” 936; ὁρᾷς ἵν᾽ ἐσμὲν αὐτοῦ πέρι τῆς ἀπορίας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 243b.}}
**I.2.c** with Verbs of motion, **whither**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.821, +others; ὁρᾷς ἵν᾽ ἥκεις 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 687, +others, 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 2.10; ιναπερ ὥρμητο 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.74.}}
**II** of circumstance, γάμος.., ἵ. χρή **at which, when**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.27}}; ἵ. μὲν ἐξῆν αὐτοῖς.., ἐνταῦθα.. **when** it was in their power, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.9.}}
**II.2** ={ἐάν}, uncertain in [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.353 (variant{ἵν᾽ ἄν}, cf. Scholia), 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 74.7 codices, variant in 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.1, and 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 176b.}}]
**B** Final _conjunction_, **that, in order that**, from {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} downwards, mostly first word in the clause, but sometimes preceded by an emphatic word, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 169d; ἵ. δή 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.26, 23.207, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.29, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 420e, 610c: never with ἄν}} or κε (if found, these particles belong to the Verb, as in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.156, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1579).}}
**B.I** general usage:
**B.I.1** with _subjunctive_,
**B.I.1.a** after primary tenses of _indicative_, also _subjunctive_ and _imperative_: _present_ _indicative_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.252, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.111, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.2.3, “Cyr.” 1.2.11, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.2}}: _perfect_ _indicative_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.203, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.129}}: _future_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.307, 4.591, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.15}}; _subjunctive_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 364; _imperative_, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.348, +others, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 61, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 880, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 297, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 341b, “Men.” 71d.}}
**B.I.1.b** after historical tenses, in similes, where the _aorist_ is gnomic, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.490 (αὔοι codices)}}; where _aorist_ is treated as equivalent to _perfect_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.99, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.580, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.91, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.4, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.26}}: when the purpose is regarded from the point of view of the speaker's present, σὲ παῖδα ποιεύμην ἵ. μοι.. λοιγὸν ἀμύνῃς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.495, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.29, 6.100, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.44, +others, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.11,12, +others}}
**B.I.1.c** after _optative_ and ἄν, when _optative_ with οὐκ ἄν is used with sense of _imperative_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.264, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.58}}; after βουλοίμην ἄν.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.12.}}
**B.I.1.d** after _imperfect_ with ἄν, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.7.}}
**B.I.2** with _optative_,
**B.I.2.a** after historical tenses, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.3, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.2, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 215, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.11, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 314c, etc.}}: after the historical _present_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 11}}: sometimes both moods, _subjunctive_ and _optative_, follow in consecutive clauses, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.77, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.76, 9.51, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.93, 49.14.}}
**B.I.2.b** after _optative_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.369, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 325; βούλοιντ᾽ ἂν ἡμᾶς ἐξολωλέναι, ἵνα.. λάβοιεν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 413.}}
**B.I.2.c** rarely after primary tenses, by a shifting of the point of view, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.250, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 24, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 410c.}}
**B.I.3** with past tenses of _indicative_,
**B.I.3.a** after unfulfilled wishes, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cri.” 44d.}}
**B.I.3.b** after _indicative_ with ἄν, to express a consequence which has not followed or cannot follow, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1389, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 89b, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 29.17: especially after ἐβουλόμην ἄν.., 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 961, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 4.3.}}
**B.I.3.c** after such Verbs as ἐχρῆν, ἔδει, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 647, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 335c, “Smp.” 181e, “Euthd.” 304e, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.5, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.48, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 349.5, etc.: whenan unfulfilled obligation is implied, τεθαύμακα ὅτι οὐκ εἶπεν (={ἔδει εἰπεῖν}).. ἵ... 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 161c}}]; ἀντὶ τοῦ κοσμεῖν (={δέον κοσμεῖν}).. ἵ... {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 36.47.}}
**B.I.3.d** after _present_ _indicative_ in general statements (including the past), οὐδὲ γὰρ τὸ εἶναι ἔχει ἡ ὕλη, ἱ. ἀγαθοῦ ταύτῃ μετεῖχεν {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 1.8.5.}}
**B.I.4** ἵ. μή as the _negative_ of ἵνα, **that not**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.348, etc.}}
**B.II** special usages:
**B.II.1** like{ὅπως}, after Verbs of command and entreaty, is common only in later Gr. (but[{{NT+8th c.BC+|compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.327 with [prev. work] 19), ἀξιοῦν ἵ... Decrees cited in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.155; δεήσεσθαι ἵ... 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.83; παρακαλεῖν ἵ... 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 3.23.27: frequently in “NT”, ἐκήρυξαν ἵ. μετανοήσωσιν NT.Mark.6.12, +others; of will, ὅσα ἐὰν θέλητε ἵ. ποιῶσιν.. [prev. work] 25: hence ἵ. with _subjunctive_ stands for infinitive, ἐν τούτῳ ἵ. καρπὸν φέρητε (={ἐν τῷ φέρειν}) NT.John.15.8, etc.; πρῶτόν ἐστιν ἵ. κοιμηθῶ 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.10.8, compare 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 8.29}}]; also for ὥστε, {{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.Gen.22.14, +others, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.333a, 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 2.33, etc.}}
**B.II.2** **because**, ἵ. ἀναγνῶ ἐτιμήθην I was honoured **because** I read, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Anonymus cited in 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 266.5, compare “Conj.” 243.21, 4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus “Scholia in Theodosii Canones” 2.257, +others}}; not found in literature.
**B.II.3** elliptical usages,
**B.II.3.a** where the purpose of the utterance is stated, Ζεὺς ἔσθ᾽, ἵν᾽ εἰδῇς 'tis Zeus,— [I tell thee this] **that** thou may'st know it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 989; ἵ. μὴ εἴπω ὅτι οὐδεμιᾷ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 507d; ἵ. συντέμω 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 45.5; ἵν᾽ ἐκ τούτων ἄρξωμαι [prev. author] 21.43}}; ἵ. δῶμεν.. **granted that.. **, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 2.34, compare 1.79.}}
**B.II.3.b** in commands, introducing a principal sentence, ἵ. συντάξῃς **order** him.., {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 240.12 (3rd c.BC)}}; ἵ. λαλήσῃς {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PSI” 4.412.1 (3rd c.BC)}}; ἵ. ἐλθὼν ἐπιθῇς τὰς χεῖρας αὐτῇ {{LXX+NT+5th c.BC+|NT.Mark.5.23,cf. NT.2Cor.8.7, LXX.2Mac.1.9, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 4.1.41, 1st c.BC: Didymus Grammaticus cited in Scholia 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 156.}}
**B.II.3.c** ἵ. τί (i.e. γένηται); **to what end?** either _absolutely_ or as a question, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 719}}; or with a Verb following, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pax” 409, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 26d, etc.}}; ἵ. δὴ τί; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1192.}}
**B.II.3.d** in indignant exclamations, **to think that.. !** Σωκράτης ἵ. πάθῃ ταῦτα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.29.16.}}
**B.III** in later Gr. with _indicative_, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Exo.1.11, +others, NT.Gal.2.4, 4.17, etc.}}

2448:
**Ἰούδα**, (a) Ἰουδαῖος, ὁ, **a Jew**: Jhn.4:9, Act.18:24, Rom.2:28; pl., Rev.2:9 3:9; οἱ Ἰ., Mat.2:2, Mrk.7:3, Jhn.2:6, al.; Ἰ. τε καὶ Ἕλληνες, Act.14:1, al.; κ. προσήλυτοι, Act.2:10; ἔθνη τε κ. Ἰ., Act.14:5; οἱ κατὰ τὰ ἔθνη, Act.21:21; of Jewish Christians, Gal.2:13; of the ruling class who opposed Jesus, Jhn.1:19 2:18 5:10 11:8 13:33, al.;
(b) Ἰουδαία, -ας, ἡ (sc. γῆ, χώρα, cf. Jhn.3:22, Mrk.1:5), (Heb. יְהוּדָה), **Judæa**: Mat.2:1, Luk.1:5, Jhn.4:3, al.
(From Abbott-Smith. LSJ has no entry)

2460:
** ἱππ-εύς**,
_genitive_ έως (-έω uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus)}}, _Epic dialect_ ῆος, ὁ, (ἵππος) **one who fights from a chariot**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (only in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad”), opposed to πεζός, 2.810}}; either of **the driver** or of **the hero who fights**, {{Refs|12.66, 15.270}}; also of **one who drives in a chariot-race**, {{Refs|23.262.}}
**2** **horseman, rider, cavalryman**, ἰππήων στρότος {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 5.1, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.88, 9.49, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 14 (_anapaest meter_), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1270a29, etc.}}; τῆς πολιτείας ἱ. a public **courier**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Aristaenetus Rhetor 1.26.}}
**3** **groom, Class.Rev.**{{Refs|27.12 (Laodicea Combusta), 24.12, “JHS” 18.108 (near Lysias).}}
**II** ἱππεῖς, in social and political sense, **knights**, forming an aristocracy in early Greek communities, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1297b18, etc.; at Eretria, [prev. work] 1306a35, “Ath.” 15.2}}; at Sparta, a royal bodyguard, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.124, compare 1.67, etc.; especially at Athens, the Second Class in Solon's constitution, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 7.3}}; later, an aristocratic corps of cavalry, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 225, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.5, 4th c.BC: Philochorus Historicus 100, etc.}}
**II.2** of the Roman **equites**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 37.8, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.24, 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 1.22, etc.}}; ἱππεὺς Ῥωμαίων,= Latin _eques Romanus,_ {{Refs|Mon.Anc.Gr. 7.17, “IG” 3.768a, “IGRom.” 3.204 (from Ancyra), “OGI” 547.2 ([prev. passage]), 645.7 (from Palmyra), probably in “IGRom.” 4.1213 (from Thyatira).}}
**III** **nimble** kind of **crab**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 525b8.}}
**IV** kind of **comet**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Plinius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor “Historia Naturalis” 2.90, 6th c.AD: Lydus, Joannes Laurentius Historicus “de Ostentis” 11.}}
**V** **girl's ornament**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus, compare “Ostr.” 323 (2nd c.BC).}}
**VI** a **measure**, πυρῶν, ἀμυγδάλων, ἀλεύρων, {{Refs|“Supp.epigram” 2.710 (from Pednelissus).}}

2461:
** ἱππικός**, ή, όν,
(ἵππος) **of a horse** or **horses**, frequently in Trag., ἱ. ἐκ πλευμόνων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 61}}; ἱ. φρυάγματα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 245, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 717, 719}}; ὀχήματα, ἄντυγες,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 740, “Aj.” 1030; φάτναι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 509.}}
**2** **of horsemen** or **chariots**, ἀγών {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.167, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.26 (_plural_); in ἱππικῶν ἀγών 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 698, ἱππικῶν is probably _neuter_ (see. below iv); δρόμος [prev. work] 754}}; ναυάγια{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 730; ἆθλον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 949a.}}
**3** = Latin _equester_, τάξις, ἀξίωμα, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 12.1, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 23}}; of persons, **of equestrian rank**, ἱ. ἄνδρες {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 3.4.20, compare “IGRom.” 3.474 (Lycia, 3rd c.AD), etc.}}
**II** **of riding** or **horsemanship, equestrian**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.3.20}}; ἱ. ἄσκησις training **in horsemanship,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 22.1042.21, +others}}; ἱ. ἡγήτωρ leader **of the knights**, {{Refs|[prev. work]3.693}}; **skilled in riding**, opposed to ἄφιππος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 350a, +others}}: _comparative_, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC(?): Satyrus Epigrammaticus 1, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Mus.” p.6}} K.: _superlative_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Tactica” 16.9.}}
**II.2** ἡ -κή (i.e. τέχνη), **horsemanship, riding**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 27, etc.}}; περὶ -κῆς, title of treatise by Xenophon; ἱ. ἐπιστήμη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 193b; ἱ. λόγοι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.3.20}}; τὰ ἱ. [πράγματα] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.124e, compare “Thg.” 126a}}; ἡ ἐμὴ ἱ. this **riding** of mine, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 24.10.}}
**III** **fit for riding**, _superlative_ -ώτατον, χρῆμα γυνή {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 677.}}
**IV** τὸ ἱ. the **horse, cavalry**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.87, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 682, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.5.29, etc.}}; τὰ ἱ. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.114.5.}}
**IV.2** **course** or **space of four stadia**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Solon” 23.}}
**IV.2.b** the **circus** at Rome, {{Refs|“Tab.Defix.Aud.” 187.59.}}
**V** _adverb_ -κῶς **like a horseman**: _superlative_ -κώτατα **with best horsemanship**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 21.7.}}

2462:
** ἵππος**, ὁ,
**horse**, ἡ, **mare**, most frequently _feminine_ in Poets; in full θήλεες ἵπποι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.269; ἵπποι θήλειαι 11.680, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.635; ἄρσενες ἵπποι 13.81, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.86, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 288b: _plural_, ἵπποι}} **team of chariot-horses**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.370, +others: frequently in dual, 5.237, 8.41, +others}}: hence, of **the chariot** itself, ἀφ᾽ ἵπποιιν, ἀφ᾽ ἵππων, from **the chariot**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.13, 19, +others; καθ᾽ ἵππων ἆλτο, ἐξ ἵππων βῆσε, [prev. work] 111, 163}}; ἵππων ἐπιβησόμενος intending to mount **his chariot**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 46}}; opposed to πεζοί, πλῆτο δὲ πᾶν πεδίον πεζῶν τε καὶ ἵππων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.267, compare 9.49; ἵπποι τε καὶ ἀνέρες 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.554; λαός τε καὶ ἵπποι 18.153}}; of riders, νῶθ᾽ ἵππων ἐπιβάντες ἐθύνεον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 286}}; frequently of race-horses, ἵ. ἀκαμαντόποδες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 3.4; ἀελλόποδες 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 7; ἀθληταί 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.63: _metaphorically_, ἁλὸς ἵ}}., of ships, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.708, compare 2nd c.AD: Secundus Gnomologus “Sententiae - FPG” 17.}}
**2** the constellation {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Pegasus”, 4th c.BC: Eudoxus Astronomus cited in 2nd c.BC: Hipparchus Astronomicus 1.2.12, 2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 27, 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 12.11.}}
**3** title of Hecate in the Mithraic cult, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 4.16.}}
**4** perhaps an instrument of torture, Latin _eculeus_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lucullus” 20 (_plural_).}}
**II** as Collective Noun, ἵππος, ἡ, **horse, cavalry**, ἡ τῶν Θεσσαλῶν ἵ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.64, etc.: always in _sing._, even with numerals, ἵ. χιλίη}} a thousand **horse**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 7.41; μυρίη [prev. passage]; μυρία, τρισμυρία, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 302, 315}}; ἡ διακοσία ἵ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.62; ἵππον ἔχω εἰς χιλίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.6.2.}}
**III** **a sea-fish**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Antimachus Colophonius Elegiacus et Epicus et 2nd c.AD: Numenius Apamensis Platonicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 7.304e}}; but ὁ ἵ. ὁ ποτάμιος **the hippopotamus**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.71, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 502a9; ὁ ἵ. τοῦ Νείλου 3rd c.AD(?): Achilles Tatius Astronomus 4.2.}}
**IV** **lewd woman**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 4.11.}}
**IV.b** **pudenda muliebria et virilia**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**V** **a complaint of the eyes**, such that they are always winking, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.611, +others (also in 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus, _accusative_ to [2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus]19.436).}}
**VI** title of ministrants ('chuckersout') in certain religious ceremonies, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“IG” 22.1368.144 (Athens, 2nd c.AD), 3.1280a.}}
**VII** in compounds, to express **anything large** or **coarse**, as in our **horse-chestnut, horse-laugh**, see at {ἱππό-κρημνος}, -λάπαθον, -μάραθον, -πορνος, -σέλινον, -τυφία, and compare βου-. (From ἴκϝος, see at {ἴκκος}; cf. Sanskrit _aśvas_, Latin _equus_: the ἴ- (in place of **e-**) and the aspirate are unexplained; the latter _accusative_ to {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Gellius, Aulus Grammaticus Latinus 2.3.2}} was confined to Attic; compare Λεύκ-ιππος, Γλαύκ-ιππος.)

2463:
Included with: ** Ἶρις**, ιδος, ἡ,
_accusative_ Ἶριν, _vocative_ Ἶρι:— “Iris”, the messenger of the gods among themselves, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.398 (never in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”), 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 780, etc. (Perh. from Ϝῖρις}}, compare ὠκέα Ἶρις {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.786, +others, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus [same place]; ὦκα δὲ Ἶρις 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.198 (Papyrus); possibly also from Ἐϝῖρις: Εἶρις is the name of a ship, “IG” 22.1611c137 (4th c.BC), but ἶρις is written in “Michel” 832 (Samos, 4th c.BC): allegorized as προφορικὸς λόγος and derived from εἴρω by “Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 2.43.)}}
**II** as _appellative_, **ἶρις**, ἡ, _genitive_ ἴριδος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 6.11.13, also εως 1st c.AD: Andromachus Poeta Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 14.43, “POxy.” 1088.34 (1st c.AD), “Geoponica” 6.8.1}}; _accusative_ ἶριν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Michel” [same place], 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.664e, ἴριδα 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Alexipharmaca” 406}}; _Epic dialect_ _dative_ _plural_ ἴρισσιν (see. below):—**rainbow**, δράκοντες.., ἴρισσιν ἐοικότες, ἅς τε Κρονίων ἐν νέφεϊ στήριξε, τέρας μερόπων ἀνθρώπων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.27, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 375a1, 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 2p.51U.}}
**II.2** any **bright-coloured circle** surrounding another body, as **the** lunar **rainbow**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 375a18}}**halo** of candle, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Signis Tempestatum” 13; round the eyes of a peacock's tail, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Dom.” 11}}; **the iris** of the eye, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus “περὶ ὀνομασίας” 24, [2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus] 14.702}}; also, **section through the ciliary region**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 10.2.}}
**II.3** **iridescent garment,** {{Refs|Michel [prev. cited]}}
**II.4** various species of the botanical genus **iris**, e.g. **the purple Iris, I. germanica** or **pallida**, εὐάνθεμον ἶριν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 4.1.9 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus); τὸ ἄνθος πολλὰς ἔχει ἐν αὑτῷ ποικιλίας 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Coloribus” 796b26, compare 1st c.AD: Plinius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor “Historia Naturalis” 21.40}}; also, **the white variety of it, I. florentina**, from the rhizome of which **the orris-root** of commerce is made, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 1.7.2, “CP” 6.11.13, etc.; ἶρις Ἰλλυρική 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.1, compare 1st c.AD: Plinius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor “Historia Naturalis” 13.14}}: in this sense some wrote it _oxytone_ **ἰρίς**, ίδος, {{Refs|12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 391.33, Scholia Nic. [prev. cited]}}
**II.5** a precious stone, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Plinius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor “Historia Naturalis” 37.136.}}

2467:
Related to: ** εἴδω**,
no _active_ _present_ in use, ὁράω being used:—_middle_, see.below {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11}}: _aorist 2_ **εἶδον** always in sense of **see** (so in _present_ and _aorist 1_ _middle_, **to be seen**, i.e. **seem**): but _perfect_ **οἶδα**, in _present_ sense, **know.** (With ἔ-ϝιδον, compare (ϝ) είδομαι, (ϝ) εῖδος, Latin _videre_; with (ϝ) οῖδα, cf. Sanskrit _véda_, Gothic _wait_, O{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **wát** 'know'.)
**A** _aorist 2_ εἶδον (late εἶδα {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Argonautica” 118), serving as}}_aorist_ to ὁράω, _Epic dialect_ ἴδον, _iterative_ ἴδεσκε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.217}}, late _Aeolic dialect_ εὔιδον {{Refs|“epigram Gr.” 990.11 (from Balbilla)}}; _imperative_ ἴδε (in _Attic dialect_ written as _adverb_ ἰδέ, **behold**! Hdn.Gr.2.23), ἴδετ; _subjunctive_ ἴδω, _Epic dialect_ ἴδωμι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.63}}; _optative_ ἴδοιμ; _infinitive_ ἰδεῖν, _Epic dialect_ ἰδέει; _participle_ ἰδών: hence, _future_ ἰδησῶ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 3.37:}} —_middle_, _aorist 2_ εἰδόμην, _Epic dialect_ ἰδόμην, in same sense, _poetry_, _Ionic dialect_, and later Prose (with _genitive_, [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 430) (so in compounds, even in _Attic dialect_ Prose, see at {ἐπ-}, προ-, ὑπ-ειδόμην)}}]; _imperative_ ἰδοῦ (frequently written as _adverb_ ἰδού, ={ἰδέ}); _subjunctive_ ἴδωμα; _optative_ ἰδοίμη; _infinitive_ ἰδέσθα; _participle_ ἰδόμενος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.88, +others:}}
**1** **see, perceive, behold**, ὀφθαλμοῖσι or ἐν ὀφθαλμοῖσι ἰδέσθαι **see** before the eyes, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.587, etc.; ἰδεῖν ἐν ὄμμασιν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1020}}; ἄγε, πειρήσομαι ἠδὲ ἴδωμαι well, I will try and **see**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.126, compare 21.159}}; **mark, observe**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.476, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.412, etc.: followed by _relative_ clause, ἴδωμ᾽ ὅτιν᾽ ἔργα τέτυκται 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.450; ἀλλ᾽ ἄγε θᾶσσον ἰδώμεθα ὅττι τάδ᾽ ἐστίν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.44}}: frequently in _infinitive_ after _substantive_ or _adjective_, θαῦμα ἰδέσθαι a marvel **to behold**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.725; οἰκτραῖσιν ἰδεῖν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 240; ἐλεινὸς ἰδεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 620a.}}
**1.b** **see** a person, i.e. **meet** him, **speak with** him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.125, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.4.15, etc.}}
**1.c** **see**, i.e. **experience**, νόστιμον ἦμαρ ἰδέσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.233, etc.; δούλειον ἦμαρ ἰδεῖν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 56; ἀέλιον ἕτερον ἰδεῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 835; τὴν δίκην ἰδεῖν [prev. author] “Ant.” 1270 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἀλόχου κουριδίης.. οὔ τι χάριν ἴδε he **saw** (i.e. **enjoyed**) not the favour of his wedded wife, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.243.}}
**2** **look**, ἰδεῖν ἐς.. **look** at or towards, {{Refs|2.271, etc.; ἰδεῖν ἐπί.. 23.143}}; πρός.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.244}}; εἰς ὦπα ἰδέσθαι **look** him in the face, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.373, etc.; κατ᾽ ἐνῶπα ἰδών 15.320; ἄντα, ἐσάντα, or ἄντην ἰδεῖν, 13.184, 17.334, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.78, etc.: qualified by}} _adverb_ or _adjective_, ὑπόδρα ἰδών **looking** askance, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.148, +others}}; ἀχρεῖον ἰδών **looking** helpless, {{Refs|2.269}}; κέρδος ἰδεῖν **look to** gain, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 541 (Lyric poetry).}}
**3** **see mentally, perceive**, ἰδέσθαι ἐν φρεσίν '**to see** in his mind's eye', {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.61, compare 4.249; ἰδεῖν τῇ διανοίᾳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 511a.}}
**3.b** **examine, investigate**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phd.” 70e, “Tht.” 192e}}; **consider**, ἴδωμεν τί λέγομεν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Grg.” 455a.}}
**II** _middle_, _present_ **εἴδομαι**, _Epic dialect_ ἐείδεται {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 25.58}}, _participle_ ἐειδόμενος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N..” 10.15:}}_aorist_ εἰσάμην, _Epic dialect_ _participle_ ἐεισάμενος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.22, +others}}:—only _Epic dialect_ and Lyric poetry, **to be seen, appear**, εἴδεται ἄστρα they **are visible, appear**,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8.559; εἰ. ἦμαρ ὑπὸ Τρώεσσι δαμῆναι 13.98; εἴσατο δέ σφι δεξιός 24.319; ὅπη τὸ Ταρτάρειον εἴδεται βάθρον “epigram Gr.” 1034.19 (from Callipolis), compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.283}}; perhaps also οὔ πῃ χροὸς εἴσατο none of the skin **was visible**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.191.}}
**II.2** with _infinitive_, **appear** or **seem** to be, τὸ δέ τοι κὴρ εἴδεται εἶναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|1.228; τοῦτό τί μοι κάλλιστον ἐνὶ φρεσὶν εἴδεται εἶναι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.11, etc.}}: with _infinitive_ omitted, οἱ τό γε κέρδιον εἴσατο θυμῷ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|19.283, etc.; οὐ μέν μοι κακὸς εἴδεται 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.472, compare 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 25.58}}; also, **look like** or **make a show of**.., εἴσατ᾽ ἴμεν ἐς Λῆμνον he **made a show of** going to Lemnos, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.283}}; εἴσατο δ᾽ ὡς ὅτε ῥινόν it **had the look as of** a shield, {{Refs|5.281.}}
**II.3** strictly middle, with _dative_, εἴσατο φθογγὴν Πολίτῃ she **made herself like** Polites in voice, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.791, compare 20.81; αὐδὴν εἰσάμενός τινι 3rd c.BC: Rhianus Epicus 50}}: especially in _participle_, **like**, εἰδομένη κήρυκι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.280, etc.; τῷ δ᾽ ὄψιν ἐειδόμενος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.15; εἰδόμενος τοκεῦσιν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 771 (Lyric poetry); φάσμα εἰδόμενόν τινι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.69.}}
**B** _perfect_, οἶδα I **know**, used as _present_: _pluperfect_ ᾔδεα (see. below), I **knew**, used as _imperfect_:—_perfect_ οἶδα, _Aeolic dialect_ ὄϊδα {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 145}}; _2nd pers._ _singular_ οἶδας once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.337, compare “hymnus ad Mercurium” 456, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 491, 6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus 89, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 67, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 780, 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 44.3 codices}}; οἶσθα elsewhere in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, _Attic dialect_, etc.; in Comedy texts also sometimes οἶσθας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 105, 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 15.11, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 348.5, compare 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 2.55}}; _plural_, ἴσμεν, _Epic dialect_, _Aeolic dialect_, and _Doric dialect_ ἴδμεν, also _Ionic dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.6, +others}}; ἴστε, ἴσασι [ῐς- {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.211, +others, but ῑς- [prev. work] 283, +others}}; οἴδαμεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.17, οἴδατε “Anthologia Graeca” 12.81 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus), οἴδᾱσι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.43, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 20.14 codices; dual, οἴδατον 5th-4th c.BC: Socratis et Socraticorum Epistulae “Ep.” 22.1: _imperative_ ἴσθι, ἴστω, _Boeotian dialect_ ἴττω, late ἰδέτω 3rd-2nd c.BC: Phalaris Epistolographus “Epistulae - Epistolographi” 122 codices: from _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἴσασι (ἴσαντι 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 53)}} were formed _Doric dialect_ _1st pers._ _singular_ ἴσᾱμι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 254, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.248}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἴσατι {{Refs|“IG” 14.644.4 (from Bruttii)}}; _1st pers._ _plural_ ἴσᾰμεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.14, ἴσαμες probably in “Dialexeis 5th c.BC” 6.12}}; Cretan dialect _3rd.pers._ _plural_ _subjunctive_ ἴθθαντι {{Refs|“GDI” 5024}}; _infinitive_ ϝισάμην{{Refs|Kohler-Ziebarth “Stadtrecht von Gortyn” 34 No.3.19}}; _participle_ ἴσας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Adverbiis;” 175.19, _dative_ _sing._ ἴσαντι 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.29, Cretan dialect _plural_ ἴθθαντες “GDI” 5024:}}_subjunctive_ εἰδῶ (εἰδέω, ἰδέω, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.235, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.236)}}, _Ionic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ εἰδέωσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 45.21 (Halic., 5th c.BC)}}; _Epic dialect_ also εἴδω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.174, +others (compare 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus Gr. 2.131), εἴδομεν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.363, εἴδετε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.17: _optative_ εἰδείην}}, _1st pers._ _plural_ εἰδεῖμεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 190b, “R.” 582a}}: _infinitive_ εἰδέναι, _Epic dialect_ ἴδμεναι, ἴδμεν, also ἰδέμεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.25}}: _participle_ εἰδώς, εἰδυῖα, _Epic dialect_ also ἰδυῖα, Elean ϝειζώς {{Refs|“Schwyzer” 409}}:—_pluperfect_ ᾔδεα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.71, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.150}}, _contraction_ ᾔδη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 18, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 511, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 119a, ᾔδησθα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.93, 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 416, etc. (but ᾔδεισθα}} frequently in codices, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 551, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 108, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 80d, +others), ᾔδεε (ν) 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.402, +others, ᾔδη 1.70, +others}} (also later _Attic dialect_, _accusative_ to {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Aristarchus Grammaticus cited in 4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus “Scholia in Theodosii Canones” 2.86), _Attic dialect_ _contraction_ ᾔδει (ν) 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1187, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 558, etc.; _Epic dialect_ 2 and _3rd.pers._ _sing._ ἠείδης, ἠείδη}} (variant -εις, - ει), {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.280, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.206}}; _Attic dialect_ also _1st pers._ _singular_ ᾔδειν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 37.24}}, _2nd pers._ _singular_ ᾔδεις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 554, etc.; _plural_, ᾔδειμεν 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.82, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Posteriora” 87b40, ᾔδεμεν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 14D. (to be read in 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1232), ᾔδειτε 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 55.9, etc. (ᾔδετε probably in 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1345)}}, _Ionic dialect_ ᾐδέατε {{LXX+6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.58 (συν-), ᾔδεισαν LXX.Gen.42.23, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 15.3.23, ᾔδεσαν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.175, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 54, etc.}}; late _Epic dialect_ ᾔδειν, ἠείδειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.65, 4.1700, also ᾖσμεν, ᾖστε, ᾖσαν, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 149.4 (probably), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 340, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 231, etc.}}; _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἴσαν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.405, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.772}}:—_future_, in this sense, εἴσομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.548, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 20, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 332, etc.; also εἰδήσω 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.327, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.234, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.44, 4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 31.5, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 108a28, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 5.78, 3rd-2nd c.BC: Apollonius Pergaeus Geometra “Conica” 1 “Praef.”, etc.}}; _infinitive_ εἰδησέμεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.257.}} —The _aorist_ and _perfect_ are usually supplied by γιγνώσκ; _aorist 1_ _infinitive_ εἰδῆσαι is found in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Acut.(Sp.”) 22, “Epid.” 6.8.25 (ἐξ-), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1156b27, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Char. Prooem.” 4; _imperative_ εἴδησον “PCair.Zen.” 36.2 (3rd c.BC)}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ _subjunctive_ εἰδήσωσιν Herzog **Koische Forschungen** No.{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|190 (2nd-1st c.BC)}}:—**know, have knowledge of, be acquainted with**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.: with _accusative_ _of things_, ὃς ᾔδη τά τ᾽ ἐόντα τά τ᾽ ἐσσόμενα πρό τ᾽ ἐόντα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.70}}; νοήματα, μήδεα οἶδε, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.122, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.363, etc.: less frequently with _accusative_ _person_, τούτους μὲν δὴ οἶδα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.551, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 365e, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 54.34, etc.}}; πρῶτος ὧν ἡμεῖς ἴδμεν the first we **know of**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.6, etc.; παλαίτατος ὧν ἀκοῇ ἴσμεν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.4: strengthened by εὖ or σάφα, εὖ τόδ᾽ ἴσθι}} **know** well, **be assured** of this, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 593; σάφ᾽ οἶδ᾽ ἐγώ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 740, etc.: frequently in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus with _neuter_}} _adjective_, to express character or disposition, ἄγρια οἶδε **has** fierceness **in his heart**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.41}}; ἀθεμίστια ᾔδη **had** law lessness **in his heart**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.189; αἴσιμα, ἄρτια ᾔδη, 14.433, 19.248}}; εἴ μοι ἤπια εἰδείη if he **were** kindly **disposed** towards me, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.73; φίλα εἰδότες ἀλλήλοισιν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.277; κεχαρισμένα, πεπνυμένα εἰδώς, 8.584, 24.442}}: with _genitive_, ὃς σάφα θυμῷ εἰδείη τεράων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.229; ὃς πάσης εἰδῇ σοφίης 15.412}}; τόξων ἐῢ εἰδώς **cunning with** the bow,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|2.718; αἰχμῆς ἐῢ εἰ. 15.525; οἰωνῶν σάφα εἰδώς 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.202; ἐῢ εἰδὼς τεκτοσυνάων 5.250; μάχης ἐῢ εἰδότε πάσης 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.823; κύνε εἰδότε θήρης 10.360; παῖδ᾽ ἔτ᾽ ἐόντ᾽ οὔ πω μάλα εἰδότε θούριδος ἀλκῆς 11.710; εἰδὼς πυγμαχίης 23.665; θεοπροπίων ἐῢ εἰδώς 6.438}}; χάριν εἰδέναι τινί **acknowledge** a debt to another, **thank** him,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|14.235, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.21, etc.: _imperative_, frequently in protestations, ἴστω νῦν Ζεὺς αὐτός}} **be** Zeus **my witness**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.329; ἴστω νῦν τόδε Γαῖα 15.36, etc.}}; _Boeotian dialect_ ἴττω Ἡρακλῆς etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 860, etc.:}}_participle_ εἰδώς, _absolutely_, **one who knows, one acquainted with the fact**, ἰδυίῃ πάντ᾽ ἀγορεύω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.365; μετ᾽ εἰδόσιν ἀγορεύειν 10.250; μακρηγορεῖν ἐν εἰδόσιν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.36, compare 3.53; μαθεῖν παρὰ τοῦ εἰδότος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 337d, etc.}}; also ἰδυίῃσι πραπίδεσσι with **knowing** mind, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.608, +others}}
**B.2** with _infinitive_, **know how** to do, οἶδ᾽ ἐπὶ δεξιά, οἶδ᾽ ἐπ᾽ ἀριστερὰ νωμῆσαι βῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|7.238, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1010, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 376}}; also, **to be in a condition, be able, have the power**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 664, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.40}}; of drugs, ὅσα λεπτύνειν οἶδε {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor Trall. “Febr.” 6}}; of a festival, οἶδε ἐκπέμπουσα δάκνειν Chor.p.124 {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}; **learn**, ἵν᾽ εἰδῇ μὴ 'πὶ τοῖς ἐμοῖς κακοῖς ὑψηλὸς εἶναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 729.}}
**B.3** with _participle_, **to know that** such and such **is** the fact, the _participle_ being in _nominative_ when it is a predicate of the Subject of the Verb, ἴσθι μοι δώσων **know that** thou wilt give, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1670; ἴστω ὑπὸ τοῦ ἀδελφεοῦ ἀποθανών 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.76; οὐ γὰρ οἶδα δεσπότας κεκτημένος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 397}}: in _accusative_ when it is predicate of the Object, τοὺς φιλτάτους γὰρ οἶδα νῷν ὄντας πικρούς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 234; τὸν Μῆδον ἴσμεν ἐκ περάτων γῆς ἐλθόντα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.69: with}}_participle_ omitted, γῆν αὐτὰ οἶδεν ἀμφότερα (i.e. ὄντα) {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 7.226a.}}
**B.4** less frequently c._accusative_ et _infinitive_, πλήθους.. ἂν σάφ᾽ ἴσθ᾽ ἕκατι βάρβαρον ναυσὶν κρατῆσαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 337, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1329; εὖ ἴσθι τοῦτον.. ἰσχυρῶς ἀνιᾶσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.44; also εὖ τόδ᾽ ἴσθι, μηδάμ᾽ ἡμέρᾳ μιᾷ πλῆθος τοσουτάριθμον ἀνθρώπων θανεῖν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 431; ἕν γ᾽ ἀκούσασ᾽ ἴσθι, μὴ ψευδῶς μ᾽ ἐρεῖν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1005.}}
**B.5** with _accusative_ followed by ὡς, ὅτι, etc., οἶδα κἀμαυτὴν ὅτι ἀλγῶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 332; ἐάν τινα εἰδῶσιν ὅτι ἄδικός ἐστι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 323b, etc.}}
**B.6** οὐκ οἶδ᾽ εἰ.. I **know** not whether, to express disbelief or doubt, sometimes with ἄν transposed, οὐκ οἶδ᾽ ἂν εἰ πείσαιμί σε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 48, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 45.7: with Verb omitted after εἰ, as οὐκ οἶδ᾽ εἴ τις ἄλλος}} **perhaps** no other, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.1, 12.10.}}
**B.7** in similar ellipses with other Conjunctions, οὐκ οἶδ᾽ ὅπως I **know** not how, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 40c b; οὐκ οἶδ᾽ ὁπόθεν [prev. author] “Cra.” 396d.}}
**B.8** οἶδα, ἴσθι are frequently parenthetic, οἶδ᾽ ἐγώ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 948; σάφ᾽ οἶδα [prev. work] 94, 963}}; also οἶδ᾽ ὅτι, οἶσθ᾽ ὅτι, ἴσθ᾽ ὅτι, πάρειμι δ᾽ ἄκων οὐχ ἑκοῦσιν, οἶδ᾽ ὅτι (i.e. πάρειμι) I **know it well**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 276}}; οἶδ᾽ ὅτι, frequently in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, as 9.1, +others; σάφ᾽ ἴσθ᾽ ὅτι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 889:}} —οἶσθ᾽ ὅ, οἶσθ᾽ ὡς, with _imperative_, are common in Trag. and Comedy texts, οἶσθ᾽ οὖν ὃ δρᾶσον; do—thou **know'st** what, i.e. make haste and do, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1158, compare “Pax” 1051, etc.}}; οἶσθ᾽ ὡς πόησο; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 543}}; also οἶσθ᾽.. ὡς νῦν μὴ σφαλῇ; {{Refs|[prev. author] “OC” 75}}; οἶσθα νῦν ἅ μοι γενέσθ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1203: rarely with the}}_future_, οἶσθ᾽ οὖν ὃ δράσεις (nisileg. δρᾶσον); {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyc.” 131, compare “Med.” 600 codices}}

2468:
** εἰμί**
(**sum**), _Aeolic dialect_ **ἔμμι** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 2.15, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 20.32}}; Cretan dialect **ἠμί** {{Refs|“GDI” 4959a}}; _2nd pers._ _singular_ εἶ, _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ εἰς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.388, +others, _Aeolic dialect_ ἔσσι}}, _Epic dialect_ and _Doric dialect_ ἐσσί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.176, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.90, 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 134; ἐσί “GDI” 4959a}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἐστί, _Doric dialect_ ἐντί {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 12(1).677 (from Rhodes), 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.17, etc.}}; _3rd.pers._ _dual_ ἐστόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.112}}; _1st pers._ _plural_ ἐσμέν, _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ εἰμέν (also in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.60), ἐμέν 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 294}}, _Doric dialect_ εἰμές {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.73, but ἠμέν “GDI” 5178.34}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ εἰσί (-ίν), _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ ἔασι (-ιν) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.73, 6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 8.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Antimachus Colophonius Elegiacus et Epicus 29, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 4.84}}, _Doric dialect_ ἐντί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.24, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 11.45, “IG” 9(1).32.22 (from Phocis), etc.: _imperative_ ἴσθι (ἔσθι 4th-5th c.BC: Hecataeus Milesius Historicus 361 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus)}}, _Epic dialect_ and Lyric poetry also in _middle_ form ἔσσο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.200, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 1.28, Macedonius Lyricus “Paean” 31, late Prose ἔσο 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.241d, 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 3.5, 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 5.12, 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “ad Marcellam - Porphyrii Opuscula” 34}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἔστω (ἤτω {{LXX+5th c.BC+|LXX.Psa.103.31, and late Inscrr., “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2664, +others; but in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 361c to be read ἴτω), _Doric dialect_ εἴτω, ἤτω, 4th c.AD: Heraclides Historicus cited in 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1411.21, Elean ἤστω “Schwyzer” 424}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἔστωσαν, but ἔστων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 502a, ὄντων [prev. author] “Lg.” 879b}}, and early _Attic dialect_ Inscrr., {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“IG” 12.22, etc. (ἔστωσαν first in 2nd c.BC, [prev. work]22.1328), _Doric dialect_ ἐόντων [prev. work]1126:}}_subjunctive_ ὦ, ᾖς, ᾖ, _Epic dialect_ ἔω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.18}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἔῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.300, +others (also ἔῃσι 2.366, +others, ᾖσι (ν) 19.202, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 294)}}, also _Boeotian dialect_ ἔνθω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“IG” 7.3172.165, μετείω 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.47 and perhaps εἴῃ 9.245, etc.}}; _Doric dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ὦντι {{Refs|“SIG” 940.3 (from Crete), ἔωντι “GDI” 5040.14 (from Hierapytna)}}, _Boeotian dialect_ ἴωνθι {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“IG” 7.3171.46 (3rd c.BC): _optative_ εἴην, -ης (εἴησθα 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 715)}}, -η, also ἔοις, ἔοι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.284, 142, +others, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.6}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ εἴοισαν Ἀρχ. Ἐφ. {{Refs|1911.133 (from Gonni)}}; _3rd.pers._ _dual_ εἴτην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 149e, “Sph.” 243e}}; _1st pers._ _plural_ εἶμεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 921 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus}}; _2nd pers._ _plural_ εἶτε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.195}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ εἶεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.372, etc., εἴησαν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.2, etc.}}; Elean ἔα, ={εἴη}, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|“SIG” 9 (6th c.BC), and σύν-εαν, ={συνεῖεν}, “GDI” 1149 (6th c.BC):}}]_infinitive_ εἶναι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus ἦναι “SIG” 306.9 (Tegea, 4th c.BC)}}; _Epic dialect_ ἔμμεναι (also _Aeolic dialect_ ἔμμεν᾽ {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 34), ἔμμεν (also 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 6.42, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 623 (Lyric poetry)), ἔμεναι, ἔμεν, also ἔμειν “SIG” 1166 (from Dodona)}}; _Doric dialect_ εἶμεν Foed. cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.77, 79, “IG” 7.1.7 (from Megara), ἦμεν “Test.Epict.” 5.16, “Tab.Heracl.” 1.75, Cretan dialect ἦμεν or ἤμην “Leg.Gort.” 1.15, +others, “GDI” 4998i 2, +others, Megarian dialect εἴμεναι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 775, εἴμειν “IG” 12(1).155.100 (from Rhodes), 14.952 (from Agrigentum); εἶν [prev. work] 12(9).211.10 (from Eretria), “SIG” 135.4 (from Olynthus), etc.:}}_participle_ ὤν, _Epic dialect_ ἐών, ἐοῦσα, ἐόν, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Cypria ἰών “Inscription Cyprian dialect” 135.23 H.}}; _Boeotian dialect_ _feminine_ ἰῶσα {{Refs|“IG” 7.3172.116 (Orchom.)}}, _Aeolic dialect_ and _Doric dialect_ _feminine_ ἔσσα {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 75.4, “IG” 4.952.2 (Epid.), 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 28.16, ἐοῖσα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.265, ἔασσα “Lyrica Alexandrina Adespota” 9, Diotogenes Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.7.62, εὖσα Erinna Lyrica 5.5}} (also _Ionic dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 5.16, εὔντων 2.85), ἐᾶσα 5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 96d, “IG” 5(1).1470.8 (from Messene), ἴαττα “Leg.Gort.” 8.47; _accusative_ _sing._ εὖντα 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.3}}; _nominative_ _singular_ εἴς in {{Refs 7th c.BC+|4th c.AD: Heraclides Historicus cited in 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1756.13, _plural_ ἔντες “Tab.Heracl.” 1.117; _dative_ _plural_ ἔντασσι [prev. work]104; _genitive_ _plural_ παρ-έντων 7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 64}}: _imperfect_ ἦν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.77, etc., _Epic dialect_ ἔον}} (also _Aeolic dialect_, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 127, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in 1787 “fragment” 3 ii 21), in _Attic dialect_ ἦ}} (uncertain in _Aeolic dialect_, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 14.9), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 77, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 61b, etc., but usually altered to ἦν in codices (and ἦν}} is required by metre in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 280), _contraction_ from _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ ἦα (8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.808, +others, “IG” 12(8).449.2 (from Thasos)}}, whence Hom.and later _Ionic dialect_ ἔᾱ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.321, +others, ἔας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.187, ἔατε [prev. author] 4.119)}}; _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἦεν, always with ν in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}; ἔην as _1st pers._ _singular_, only {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.762 (assuming variant, +others ἔον), frequently as _3rd.pers._ _sing._ (generally before a consonant, so that ἔεν is possible), sometimes also ἤην}}; _2nd pers._ _singular_ ἦσθα, later ἦς (which is variant in {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 1.26), sometimes in LXX (LXX.Judg.11.35, LXX.Ruth.3.2, +others), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 365e, Erinna Lyrica 4.4, NT.Matt.25.21, +others, ἦσθας 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 156, _Epic dialect_ ἔησθα}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἦν, _Epic dialect_ ἔην, ἤην, ἦεν (see. above), _Doric dialect_ and _Aeolic dialect_ ἦς {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 30.1, 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 102, 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 59, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.90, “SIG” 241.145 (Delph.)}}; _3rd.pers._ _dual_ ἤστην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.10, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 387, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 982, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 272a, +others}}; _Doric dialect_ _1st pers._ _plural_ ἦμες {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 21}}; _2nd pers._ _plural_ ἦτε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 276c, ἦστε 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 821, “Ec.” 1086}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἦσαν, _Ionic dialect_ and poetical ἔσαν (in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 321, 825, ἦν is not _plural_ for ἦσαν}}, but is rather a peculiarity of syntax, see below see, but is _3rd.pers._ _plural_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 46, +others, “SIG” 560.15 (Epidamnus, 3rd c.BC))}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἔον {{NT+5th c.BC+|“Schwyzer” 644.12; later ἤμην “PSI” 4.362.21 (3rd c.BC), “SIG” 527.46 (Crete, 3rd c.BC), “IGRom.” 4.1740 (from Cyme), always in LXX as LXX.Bar.1.19, cf. NT.Matt.23.30, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.174a, etc., and sometimes in codices of earlier writers, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.34, “Tragica Adespota” 124 (cited from 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 931 by 4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus and from [prev. author] “Tr.” 474 by Aps.), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.9, 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “adv. Athenogenem” 26}}, _2nd pers._ _singular_ ἦσο {{Refs|“epigram Gr.” 379 (from Aezani)}}, _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἦστο {{Refs|“Supp.epigram” 1.455.7 (from Phrygia)}}, _1st pers._ _plural_ ἤμεθα {{NT+3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 2p.11 (3rd c.BC), LXX.Bar.1.19, LXX.1Ki.25.16, NT.Eph.2.3}}; _subjunctive_ ὦμαι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PBaden” 48.12 (2nd c.BC), ἦται “GDI” , ἦνται probably in “IG” 5(1).1390.83 (from Andania)}}; _Ionic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ also ἔσκον, used by {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 656 (Lyric poetry):}}_future_ ἔσομαι, ἔσται, _Epic dialect_ and _Aeolic dialect_ also ἔσσομαι, ἔσεται, ἔσσετα; _Aeolic dialect_ _2nd pers._ _singular_ ἔσσῃ probably in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 67, 87; _Doric dialect_ 2 and _3rd.pers._ _sing._ ἐσσῇ, ἐσσεῖται, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.393, 13.317, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 10.5, _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἐσσοῦνται Foed. cited in 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.77 codices (but ἔσσονται “Tab.Heracl.” 1.113)}}, _infinitive_ ἐσσεῖσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 57.}} —All forms of the _present_ _indicative_ are enclitic (except _2nd pers._ _singular_ εἶ and _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἔασι); but _3rd.pers._ _singular_ is written ἔστι when it begins a sentence or verse, or when it immediately follows οὐκ, καί, εἰ, ὡς, ἀλλά, or τοῦτ᾽, Hdn.Gr.{{Refs|1.553 (also μή _accusative_ to “Etymologicum Magnum” 301.3)}}; later Grammars wrote ἔστι as _substantive_ Verb, {{Refs 9th c.AD+|9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc., 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 880.22.}}
**A** as the _substantive_ Verb,
**A.I** of persons, **exist**, οὐκ ἔσθ᾽ οὗτος ἀνήρ, οὐδ᾽ ἔσσεται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.437}}; ἔτ᾽ εἰσί they **are** still **in being**,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|15.433, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 445, etc.; τεθνηῶτος.. μηδ᾽ ἔτ᾽ ἐόντος 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.289}}; οὐκέτ᾽ ἐστί he **is** no more, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1162}}; οὐδὲ δὴν ἦν he **was** not long-**lived**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.131}}; ὁ οὐκ ὤν, οἱ οὐκ ὄντες, of **those who are** no more, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.45, 44}}; οἱ ὄντες the **living**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.29.2}}; ὁ ὤν the {{LXX+8th c.BC+|“Eternal”, LXX.Exo.3.14, +others, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.289; θεοὶ αἰὲν ἐόντες 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.290}}; ἐσσόμενοι **posterity**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|2.119; κἀγὼ γὰρ ἦ ποτ᾽, ἀλλὰ νῦν οὐκ εἴμ᾽ ἔτι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 284}}; ὡς ἂν εἶεν ἅνθρωποι **might continue in being**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 190c; ζώντων καὶ ὄντων Ἀθηναίων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.72, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione et Corruptione” 318b25}}; of things, εἰ ἔστι ἀληθέως [ἡ τράπεζα] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.17, etc.}}; of cities, ὄλωλεν, οὐδ᾽ ἔτ᾽ ἐστὶ Τροία {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1292, compare “Heracl.” 491}}; δοκεῖ μοι Καρχηδόνα μὴ εἶναι **censeo Carthaginem esse delendam**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Ma.” 27}}; ἂν ᾖ τὸ στράτευμα **be in existence**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.17}}; of money, **to be in hand**, τῶν ὄντων χρημάτων καὶ τῶν προσιόντων {{Refs|“IG” 12.91.25}}; τὰ ὄντα **property**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 511a, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 24, etc.}}; τὸ ἐσόμενον ἐκ.. **future revenue** from.., {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BCH” 46.420 (Olymos, 1st c.BC)}}; of place, τὴν οὖσαν ἐκκλησίαν the **local** church, {{NT|NT.Act.13.1}}; of time, τοῦ ὄντος μηνός in the **current** month, {{Refs|“BGU” 146.4, etc.}}; **in office**, ἱερέων τῶν ὄντων {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PPar.” 5.4 (2nd c.BC)}}; αἱ οὖσαι [ἐξουσίαι] the powers **that be, Ep. Rom.**{{Refs|13.1.}}
**A.II** of the **real** world, **be**, opposed to become, γίγνεται πάντα ἃ δή φαμεν εἶναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 152d, etc.}}; τὸ ὄν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Being”, 4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 8.35, 5th c.BC: Protagoras Philosophus 2, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 27d, etc.}}; opposed to τὸ μὴ ὄν, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Gorgias Rhetor et Sophista “fragment” 3 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, etc.; οὐδὲν γίνεται ἐκ τοῦ μὴ ὄντος 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.5U.; ἐξ οὐκ ὄντων ἐποίησεν αὐτὰ ὁ θεός LXX.2Mac.7.28}}; τὰ ὄντα the **world of things**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 7, 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 129.5, etc.}}; ὄν_indeclinable_, τῶν ὂν εἰδῶν species **of Being**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.2.10.}}
**A.II.2** of circumstances, events, etc., **to happen**, τά τ᾽ ἐόντα, τά τ᾽ ἐσσόμενα, πρό τ᾽ ἐόντα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.70; ἡ ἐσβολὴ ἔμελλεν ἔσεσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.13, etc.}}; τῆς προδοσίας οὔσης since treachery **was there**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.103}}; ἕως ἂν ὁ πόλεμος ᾖ so long as it **last**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.58; αἱ σπονδαὶ ἐνιαυτὸν ἔσονται [prev. author] 4.118}}; τί ἐστι; what **is** it? what's the matter? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 193}}; τί οὖν ἦν τοῦτ; how **came** it **to pass**? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 58a: repeated with a _relative_ to avoid a positive assertion, ἔστι δ᾽ ὅπῃ νῦν ἔ.}} things **are** as they **are**, i.e. are ill, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 67.}}
**A.III** **be the fact** or **the case**, διπλασίαν ἂν τὴν δύναμιν εἰκάζεσθαι ἤ ἐστιν twice as large as it **really is**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.10}}; αὐτὸ ὅ ἐστι καλόν beauty **in its essence**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 211c, compare “Phd.” 74b}}; frequently in _participle_, τὸν ἐόντα λόγον λέγειν or φαίνειν the **true** story, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.95, 116}}; τῷ ἐόντι χρήσασθαι tell the **truth**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 30; τὰ ὄντα ἀπαγγέλλειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.8}}; σκῆψιν οὐκ οὖσαν, λόγον οὐκ ὄντα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 584, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1052}}; τῷ ὄντι **in reality, in fact**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 328d, etc.}}; to apply a quotation to a case in point, τῷ ὄντι κλαυσίγελως **real** 'smiles through tears' (with allusion to {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.484), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.2.9, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 196d}}; κατὰ τὸ ἐόν according to the **fact, right**ly, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.97}}; πᾶν τὸ ἐόν the whole **truth**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 9.11; τοῦ ἐόντος ἀποτεύξεται 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 2.}}
**A.IV** followed by the _relative_, οὐκ ἔστιν ὅς or ὅστις **no one**, οὐκ ἔσθ᾽ ὃς.. ἀπαλάλκοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.348; οὐκ ἔ. οὐδεὶς ὅς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 903}}; οὐκ ἔ. ὅτῳ, ={οὐδενί}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 293 (_anapaest meter_), compare 989: frequently in _plural_, εἰσὶν οἵ}}, = Latin _sunt qui_, used exactly like{ἔνιοι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.88, 7.44, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 77d, “Grg.” 503a, etc. (εἰσί τινες οἵ.. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.24)}}; ἐστὶν ἃ χωρία, πολίσματα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.12, 65; ἐστὶν ἃ εἰπεῖν [prev. author] 2.67; ἦσαν οἵ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.2.14}}; the _singular_ Verb is used even with _masculine_ and _feminine_ _plural_, ἐστὶν οἵ, αἵ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 1, “VC” 4, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.3.16}}; more frequently in oblique cases, ποταμῶν ἐστὶ ὧν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.187; ἐστὶν ἀφ᾽ ὧν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.65}}; ἐστὶ παρ᾽ οἷς, ἐστὶν ἐν οἷς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.23, 5.25: in questions ὅστις is used, ἔστιν ἥντινα δόξαν.. ἀπεκρίνατ; 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 85b: with _relative_ Particles, ἐστὶν ἔνθα}}, = Latin _est ubi_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.4.15, etc.}}; ἐ. ὅπῃ, ἔσθ᾽ ὅπου, **some**how, **some**where, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 331d, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 517, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 448, etc.}}; in questions expecting a _negative_ answer, ἐ. ὁπόθεν, ὅπω; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 35a, “R.” 493e, etc.; οὐ γάρ ἐσθ᾽ ὅπως 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 61, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.102, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 620}}; οὐκ ἔ. ὅπως οὐ **in any case, necessarily**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 188; οὐκ ἔ. ὡς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 76e, etc.}}; ἐ. ὅτε, ἔσθ᾽ ὅτε, **some**times, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 180.2, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 56, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.21, etc.}}
**A.V** ἦν is sometimes used with _plural_ _masculine_ and _feminine_, usually at the beginning of a sentence, **there was**, τῆς δ᾽ ἦν τρεῖς κεφαλαί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 321; (but in ἦν δ᾽ ἐρῳδιοί τε πολλοί 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 46, compare 59, +others, it may be taken as _Doric dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_)}}; ἦν δ᾽ ἀμφίπλεκτοι κλίμακες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 520 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἦν ἄρα κἀκεῖνοι ταλακάρδιοι epigram cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.184; less frequently ἔστι, ἔστι δὲ μεταξὺ.. ἑπτὰ στάδιοι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.26, compare 7.34; ἔστι.. ἄρχοντές τε καὶ δῆμος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 463a}}; before dual Nouns, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 58, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 500d.}}
**A.VI** ἔστι _impersonal_, with _infinitive_, **it is possible**, ἔστι γὰρ ἀμφοτέροισιν ὀνείδεα μυθήσασθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.246; ἔστι μὲν εὕδειν, ἔστι δὲ τερπομένοισιν ἀκούειν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.392}}; εἴ τί πού ἐστι (i.e. πιθέσθαι){{Refs 5th c.BC+|4.193; τοιάδε.. ἐστὶν ἀκοῦσαι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1055 (_anapaest meter_); ἔστι τεκμήρια ὁρᾶν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.2.13, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1163, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.105, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.272, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 53.6, etc.}}; so in _imperative_, _optative_, and _subjunctive_, ἔστω ἀποφέρεσθαι τῷ βουλομένῳ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“IG” 12.10.7; μυρία ἂν εἴη λέγειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 271e; ὅπως ἂν ᾖ δρᾶν “IG” 2.1054.91: more frequently in _negative_ clauses, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.267, etc.}}; followed by ὥστε with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 656}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, ἁδόντα δ᾽ εἴη με τοῖς ἀγαθοῖς ὁμιλεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.96; ἔστιν ἐκπεσεῖν ἀρχῆς Δία 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 757}}: sometimes not _impersonal_ in this sense, θάλασσα δ᾽ οὐκέτ᾽ ἦν ἰδεῖν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pers.” 419.}}
**A.VI.b** ἔστω in argument, **let it be granted**, ἔστω τοῦτο ἀληθὲς εἶναι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 25; ἔστω σοι τοῦθ᾽ οὕτως 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.987b; ἔστω εἶναί τινα τοιοῦτον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator Chr.74.24.}}
**B** most frequently, **to be**, the Copula connecting the predicate with the Subject, both being in the same case: hence, **signify, import**, τὸ γὰρ εἴρειν λέγειν ἐστίν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 398d}}; especially in the phrase τοῦτ᾽ ἔστι, **hoc est**; Σκαιόλαν, ὅπερ ἐστὶ Λαϊόν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Publicola” 17: with numerals, τὰ δὶς πέντε δέκα ἐστίν}} twice five **are** ten, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.4.7}}; εἶναί τις or τι, **to be** somebody, something, **be** of some consequence, see at {τι; οὐδὲν εἶναι} {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 562d, etc.}}
**B.2** _periphrastic_ with the Participle to represent the finite Verb: with _perfect_ _participle_ once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, τετληότες εἰμέν, for τετλήκαμεν, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.873}}; so in Trag. and _Attic dialect_, ἦν τεθνηκώς, for ἐτεθνήκει, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 869}}; ἔσται δεδορκώς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1179; εἰμὶ γεγώς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1299; πεφυκός ἐστι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1473; δεδρακότες εἰσίν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.68; κατακεκονότες ἔσεσθε 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.6.36}}: with _aorist_ _participle_, once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, βλήμενος ἦν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.211}}; so προδείσας εἰμί, οὐ σιωπήσας ἔσε; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 90, 1146, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 460}}: with _present_ _participle_, ἦν προκείμενον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pers.” 371; φεύγων Ὀρέστης ἐστίν [prev. author] “Ch.” 136; εἴην οὐκ ἂν εὖ φρονῶν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1330}}; τί δ᾽ ἐστί.. φέρο; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “OT” 991, compare 274, 708; λέγων ἐστίν τις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1179; ἦν τίς σ᾽ ὑβρίζων [prev. author] “HF” 313; πόρρω ἤδη εἶ πορευόμενος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 204b; βαδίζων εἰμί 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 36; frequently in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, ἦσαν ἱέντες 1.57, +others; even εἰσὶ διάφοροι ἐόντες 3.49 (assuming variant):}} — if the _Article_ is joined with the Part., the noun is made emphatic, Κᾶρές εἰσι οἱ καταδέξαντες the persons who showed her **were** Carians, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.171; αὐτὸς ἦν ὁ μαρτυρῶν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 798; δόλος ἦν ὁ φράσας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 197 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**C** εἶναι is frequently modified in sense by the addition of _adverbs_, or the cases of Nouns without or with Preps.:
**C.I** εἶναι with _adverbs_, where the _adverb_ often merely represents a Noun and stands as the predicate, ἅλις δέ οἱ ἦσαν ἄρουραι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.122, etc.}}; ἀκέων, ἀκήν εἶναι, **to be** silent,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|4.22, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.82; σῖγα πᾶς ἔστω λεώς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 532; διαγνῶναι χαλεπῶς ἦν ἄνδρα ἕκαστον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.424}}; ἀσφαλέως ἡ κομιδὴ ἔσται **will go on** safely, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.134}}; ἐγγύς, πόρρω εἶναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.88, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 356e: frequently _impersonal_ with words implying good or ill fortune, Κουρήτεσσι κακῶς ἦν}} **it fared** ill with them, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.551; εὖ γὰρ ἔσται 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 89, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1188, etc.; ἡδέως ἂν αὐτοῖς εἴη 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.30.}}
**C.II** with _genitive_, to express descent or extraction, πατρὸς δ᾽ εἴμ᾽ ἀγαθοῖο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.109; αἵματός εἰς ἀγαθοῖο 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.611, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.71, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.71, etc.; πόλεως μεγίστης εἶ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.3.19.}}
**C.II.b** to express the material of which a thing is made, ἡ κρηπίς ἐστι λίθων μεγάλων **consists of**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.93; τῆς πόλιος ἐούσης δύο φαρσέων [prev. work] 186}}; τοιούτων ἔργων ἐστὶ ἡ τυραννίς **is made up of**.., {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.92}}.ή, etc.
**C.II.c** to express the class to which a person or thing belongs, εἶ γὰρ τῶν φίλων **you are one of** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 345; ἐτύγχανε βουλῆς ὤν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.70; ὅσοι ἦσαν τῶν προτέρων στρατιωτῶν [prev. author] 7.44; Κριτίας τῶν τριάκοντα ὤν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.31}}; ἔστι τῶν αἰσχρῶν **it is in the class of** disgraceful things, i. e. **it is** disgraceful, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.2.}}
**C.II.d** to express that a thing belongs to another, Τροίαν Ἀχαιῶν οὖσαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 269; τὸ πεδίον ἦν μέν κοτε Χορασμίων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.117, etc.}}: hence, **to be of the party of**, ἦσαν.. τινὲς μὲν φιλίππου, τινὲς δὲ τοῦ βελτίστου {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.56, compare 37.53}}; **to be de pendent upon**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 737, etc.}}; **to be at the mercy of**, ἔστι τοῦ λέγοντος, ἢν φόβους λέγῃ {{Refs|[prev. author] “OT” 917.}}
**C.II.e** to express one's duty, business, custom, nature, and the like, οὔτοι γυναικός ἐστι **'tis** not a woman's **part**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 940; τὸ ἐπιτιμᾶν παντὸς εἶναι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.16}}; τὸ δὲ ναυτικὸν τέχνης ἐστίν **is matter of** art, **requires** art, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.142, compare 83.}}
**C.II.f** in LXX, **to be occupied about**, ἦσαν τοῦ θύειν {{LXX|LXX.2Ch.30.17}}; ἔσεσθαι, with _genitive_, **to be about to**, ἐσόμεθα τοῦ σῶσαί σε {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.10.11.}}
**C.III** with the _dative_, ἐστί μοι I **have**, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.}}
**C.III.2** with two datives., σφίσι τε καὶ Ἀθηναίοισι εἶναι οὐδὲν πρῆγμα that they and the Athenians **have** nothing **to do** one with another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.84; μηδὲν εἶναι σοὶ καὶ φιλίππῳ πρᾶγμα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.283}}; more shortly, σοί τε καὶ τούτοισι πρήγμασι τί ἐστ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.33}}; τί τῷ νόμῳ καὶ τῇ βασάν; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 29.36}}; τί ἐμοὶ καὶ σο; Latin _quid tecum est mihi?_ {{NT|NT.Mark.5.7, etc.}}; also ἐμοὶ οὐδὲν πρὸς τοὺς τοιούτους (i.e. ἐστίν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.12; ἐν οἷς πρὸς τοὺς ἐναντίους ἐστὶ τῷ δήμῳ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.278}}; ἔσται αὐτῳ πρὸς τὸν θεόν, in tomb inscriptions, {{Refs|“JHS” 18.113, etc.}}
**C.III.3** with ἄσμενος, βουλόμενος, etc., added, ἐμοὶ δέ κεν ἀσμένῳ εἴη **'twould be** to my delight, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.108; οὐκ ἂν σφίσι βουλομένοις εἶναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.35; προσδεχομένῳ [prev. author] 6.46; θέλοντι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1356 (Lyric poetry); ἡδομένοις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 187c.}}
**C.IV** with Preps., εἶναι ἀπό τινος, ={εἶναί τινος}{{Refs 5th c.BC+|(above 11.a), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.6.9; εἰσὶν ἀπ᾽ ἐναντίων αὗται πραγμάτων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 12d}}; but εἶναι ἀπ᾽ οἴκου **to be** away from.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.99.}}
**C.IV.2** εἶναι ἔκ τινος **to be sprung** from, εἴμ᾽ ἐκ Παιονίης, Μυρμιδόνων ἔξ εἰμι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.154, 24.397, etc.}}; ἔστιν ἐξ ἀνάγκης **it is** of necessity, i. e. necessary, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 256d.}}
**C.IV.3** εἶναι ἐν.. **to be** in a certain state, ἐν εὐπαθείῃσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.22}}; ἐν ἀθυμία, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.46, etc.; ἐν ταραχαῖς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.218}}; εἶναι ἐν ἀξιώματι **to be** in esteem, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.130}}; οἱ ἐν τέλεϊ ἐόντες those in office, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.18, etc.}}; but εἶναι ἐν τέχνῃ, ἐν φιλοσοφία **to be engaged** in.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 562, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 59a.}}
**C.IV.3.b** ἐν σοί ἐστι **it depends** on thee, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.109, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 963; ἐν σοὶ γάρ ἐσμεν [prev. author] “OT” 314; so also ἐπί τινι [prev. author] “Ph.” 1003, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.2, etc.}}
**C.IV.4** εἶναι διά.., much like{εἶναι ἐν}.., εἶναι διὰ φόβου, ={φοβεῖσθαι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.34}}; εἶναι δι᾽ ὄχλου, ={ὀχληρὸν εἶναι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.73; εἶναι διὰ μόχθων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.25}}; εἶναι δι᾽ αἰτίας, ={αἰτιᾶσθαι}, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.70}}; Geometry texts, **pass** through, διὰ τᾶς ἑτέρας διαμέτρου ἐόντος τοῦ ἐπιπέδου {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ κωνοειδέων καὶ σφαιροειδέων” 20.}}
**C.IV.5** εἶναι ἐφ᾽ ἑαυτῆς **to be** by oneself, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.23}}; εἶναι ἐπὶ ὀνόματος **to bear** a name, {{Refs|[prev. author] 39.21}}; εἶναι ἐπὶ τοῖς πράγμασιν **to be engaged** in.., {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.12}}; εἶναι ἐπί τινα **to be** against him, {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.33}}; εἶναι ἐφ᾽ ἑξήκοντα στάδια **to reach** sixty stadia, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.6.11}}; εἶναι ἐπὶ τὰς ἁφάς **pass** through the points of contact, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Biographus Perg. “Con.” 4.1}}; εἶναι ἐπί τινι, see above 3 b.
**C.IV.6** εἶναι πρός τινος **to be** in one's favour, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.10, 29, etc.}}; **to suit**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.11, etc.}}; εἶναι πρός τινι **engaged** in, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 84c, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 5.31}}; πρὸς τοῖς ἰδίοις **mind** one's own affairs, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1309a6, “Ath.” 16.3; εἶναι πρὸς τὸ κωλύειν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.26.3}}; πρὸς τὸ πονεῖν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Teles p.46 H.; εἶναι περί τι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.5.7, etc.}}
**C.IV.7** εἶναι παρά τινι or τινα, = παρειναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 6.2.15, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.140.ά}} (assuming variant).
**C.IV.8** εἶναι ὑπό τινα or τινι **to be** subject to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.2.17 (assuming variant), 6.2.4.}}
**C.IV.9** περὶ τούτων ἐστίν that **is** the question, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 30.}}
**C.IV.10** εἶναι ἀπό.., in Geometry texts, **to be constructed** upon, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ σφαίρας καὶ κυλίνδρου” 2.9, “Con.Sph.” 7.}}
**D** ἐστί is very frequently omitted, mostly in the _present_ _indicative_ before certain predicates, as ἀνάγκη, ἄξιον, δυνατόν, εἰκός, ἕτοιμον, οἷόν τε, ῥᾴδιον, χρεών, etc., and after the _neuter_ of Verbals in -τέος, and such forms as θαυμαστὸν ὅσον: less frequently with other persons and moods, εἰμί omitted, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 92, “Aj.” 813}}; εἶ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.206}}; ἐσμέν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 634}}; ἐστέ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.463}}; εἰσί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 499 (Lyric poetry), “IG” 2.778 B}}; _subjunctive_ ᾖ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.376, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 659, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.32}}; _optative_ εἴη, {{Refs|“IG” 22.1183.12}}; _imperfect_ ἦν, {{Refs|[prev. work]2.778 B}}; _future_ ἔσονται, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.394.}}
**E** the Inf. frequently seems redundant,
**E.1** in phrases implying power or will to do a thing, ἑκὼν εἶναι (see. ἑκών) κατὰ δύναμιν εἶναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 2.32; εἰς δύναμιν εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 300c}}; τὸ ἐπ᾽ ἐκείνοις εἶναι, **quantum in illis esset**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.48, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.5.9, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.58; τὸ ἐπὶ σφᾶς εἶναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.28; τὸ κατὰ τοῦτον εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.6.9; κατὰ τοῦτο εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 317a}}; τὸ τήμερον, τὸ νῦν εἶναι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Cra.” 396e, “La.” 201c, Theopomp. Comedy texts 98, Decrees cited in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 31.2, etc.}}
**E.2** after Verbs of naming or choosing, σοφιστὴν ὀνομάζουσι τὸν ἄνδρα εἶναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 311e; σύμμαχόν μιν εἵλοντο εἶναι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.134}}; of giving, δῶκε ξεινήϊον εἶναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.20.}}
**F** _imperfect_ ἦνissts. used where other languages take the _present_,
**F.1** after ἄρα, to express a fact which **is and has always been** the same, δέρμα δὲ ἀνθρώπου.. ἦν ἄρα σχεδὸν δερμάτων πάντων λαμπρότατον human skin then **it appears is**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.64; Κύπρις οὐκ ἄρ᾽ ἦν θεός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 359}}; ὡς ἄρ᾽ ἦσθ᾽ ἐμὸς πατὴρ ὀρθῶς{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. work] 1169; ἦ πολύμοχθον ἄρ᾽ ἦν γένος.. ἁμερίων [prev. author] “IA” 1330; ἦ στωμύλος ἦσθα 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.79}}; so also when there is reference to a past thought, τουτὶ τί ἦ; what **is** this? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 157, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 387c: so in the Aristotelian formula τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι (“APo.” 82b38, +others)}}, used to express **the essential nature** of a thing, where τί ἦν (for ἐστί) takes the place of the _dative_ in such phrases as τὸ ἀγαθῷ εἶναι, τὸ μεγέθει εἶναι, {{Refs|“APr.” 67b12, “de An.” 429b10.}}
**G** ἐγώ εἰμι, in {{LXX|LXX, pleonastic for ἐγώ, ἐγώ εἰμι οὐχ ἥμαρτον LXX.Judg.11.27, compare 6.18; also ἔσται πᾶς ἀποκτενεῖ με LXX.Gen.4.14.}}

2470:
** ἴσος**, η, ον,
_Epic dialect_ **ἶσος** and **ἔϊσος** (see. below); Cretan dialect, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus}} **ϝίσϝος** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|“GDI” 4998ii2, 4982.2, “Schwyzer” 665, compare γισγόν· ἴσον, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}; later **ἵσος** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Schwyzer” 708a (1) (Ephesus, 4th c.BC), “Tab.Heracl.” 1.175, etc.}}:— **equal** in size, strength, or number, with _dative_, κύματα ἶσα ὄρεσσιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.290, etc.}}; frequently of appearance, **like**, ἶσος ἀναύδῳ {{Refs 7th c.BC+|10.378; ἶσος Ἄρευι 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 91 (uncertain)}}; ἴσος θεοῖσιν Ead.2.1: frequently _absolutely_, ἴσην.. βίην καὶ κῦδος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.205}}; ἶσον θυμὸν ἔχειν to be of **like** mind, {{Refs|13.704, 17.720: _neuter_ as}} _adverb_, ἶσον ἐμοὶ φρονέουσα {{Refs|15.50; θεοῖσιν ἶσ᾽ ἔθελε φρονέειν 5.441, compare 21.315}}, etc.; ἴσος τινὶ τὸ μέγαθος, ὕψος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.32, 124}}; τὸ μῆκος, τὸ πλάτος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.4.32; ἀριθμόν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 662}}; ἴσα τὸν ἀ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 441c}}; ποτὴν ἴσον **equal** in flight of song, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Alexander Aetolus Elegiacus 5.5}}; ἴσον, τό, **copy** of a document, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“PLond.” 3.1222.5 (2nd c.AD), etc.: with _dative_ _person_ in place of an object of comparison, οὐ μὲν σοί ποτε ἶσον ἔχω γέρας (i.e. τῷ σῷ γέραϊ) 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.163}}; τοῖσδ᾽ ἴσας ναῦς (i.e. ταῖς τῶνδε) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 262 (Lyric poetry); ἴσα τοῖς νῦν στρατηγοῖς ἀγάθ᾽ εἰργασμένους 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 13.21: followed by a relative word, ἐμοὶ ἴσον.., ὅσονπερ ὑμῖν}} **the same** to me as to you, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 173; τὰ ἐκεῖ ἴσα, ὥσπερ τὰ ἐνθάδε 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.36 codices (perhaps σᾶ)}}; τὰ ἴσα ὅσαπερ.. Legal cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.44; ἴσον.. ὅπερ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Eryxias” 405b.}}
**2** repeated to denote equal relations, ἴσα πρὸς ἴσα **tit** for **tat**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.2; ταχθέντες ἴσοι πρὸς ἴσους 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 142 (_anapaest meter_); ἴσους ἴσοισι.. ἀντιθείς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 750; ἴσα ἀντὶ ἴσων λαμβάνειν, ἐκδοῦναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 774c}}; ἴσος ἴσῳ (i.e. οἶνος ὕδατι) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 184, “Comica Adespota” 107, etc.}}; κύλικος ἴσον ἴσῳ κεκραμένης (where ἴσον is adverbial) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1132; διδόναι γάλα καὶ οἶνον πίνειν ἴσον ἴσῳ 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 2.5.1: _metaphorically_, 'fairly blended', μηδὲν ἴσον ἴσῳ φέρων 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 354.}}
**3** of persons, **equal in rights**, βούλεται ἡ πόλις ἐξ ἴσων εἶναι καὶ ὁμοίων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1295b25; ἡ πολιτικὴ ἐλευθέρων καὶ ἴσων ἀρχή [prev. work] 1255b20; τὸ κατ᾽ ἀξίαν ἴ. [prev. work] 1307a26, +others}}
**II** **equally divided** or **distributed**, ἴση μοῖρα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.318}}; ἴση alone, **one's equal share**, μή τίς μοι ἀτεμβόμενος κίοι ἴσης {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.42 (ἴσσης conjecture Fick, compare ἴσσασθαι); τὴν ἴ. ἔχων 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 250}}; οὐ μὴν ἴ. ἔτεισεν (i.e. τίσιν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 810}}; ἄχρι τῆς ἴ. up to **the point of equality**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 5.17: _neuter_, μὴ ἴσον νεῖμαι ἑκατέρῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 337a; οὐ μόνον ἴσον, ἀλλὰ καὶ πλέον ἔχειν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 17.57}}; οὐκ ἀνέξῃ δωμάτων ἔχων ἴσον καὶ τῷδε νεῖμα; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 547}}; τὰ ἴσα **fair measure**, τὰ ἴ. νέμειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.11}}; μὴ ἴσων ἕκαστον τυγχάνειν ἀλλὰ πλεονεκτεῖν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.2.20}}; προστυχεῖν τῶν ἴ. to obtain **fair terms**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 552}}; κἂν ἴσαι (i.e. ψῆφοι) γένωνται **equally divided**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 685.}}
**II.2** **based on equality of rights**, ἴ. καὶ ἔννομος πολιτεία {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.5; τὴν πολιτείαν ἰσαιτέραν καθιστάναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.89}}; τὰ ἴ. **equal rights, equality**, frequently joined with τὰ ὅμοια or τὰ δίκαια, ὡς τῆς πολιτείας ἐσομένης ἐν τοῖς ἴ. καὶ ὁμοίοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.1.45; τῶν ἴ. καὶ τῶν δικαίων ἕκαστος ἡγεῖται ἑαυτῷ μετεῖναι ἐν τῇ δημοκρατίᾳ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.67; οὐ μέτεστι τῶν ἴ. οὐδὲ τῶν ὁμοίων πρὸς τοὺς πλουσίους τοῖς λοιποῖς [prev. work]112; τῶν ἴ. μετεῖχε τοῖς ἄλλοις [prev. work]96; also ἡ ἴ. καὶ ὁμοία (i.e. δίκη), τῆς ἴ. καὶ ὁμοίας μετέχειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.105}}; ἐπ᾽ ἴ. τε καὶ ὁμοίῃ on **fair** and equal terms, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.7, ά,compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.145}}; ἐπὶ τῇ ἴ. καὶ ὁμοίᾳ{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 27, compare “SIG” 312.27 (Samos, 4th c.BC), “OGI” 229.44 (Smyrna, 3rd c.BC)}}, etc.: generally, **just, fair**, ἐκ ποίας ἴ. καὶ δικαίας προφάς εω; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.284.}}
**II.3** of persons, **fair, impartial**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 684 (Lyric poetry), “OT” 677; ἴ. δικαστής 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 957c; ἴ. καὶ κοινοὶ ἀκροαταί 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 29.1, compare 18.7; ἴ. καὶ κοινὸν δικαστήριον [prev. author] 7.36; κοινοὺς μὲν.., ἴ. δὲ μή 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 337a; ἴ. ἴσθι κρινων 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 266, compare 257; κριταὶ ἴ. καὶ δίκαιοι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 24.15.3, etc.}}
**II.4** **adequate**, ἡ ἴ. φρουρά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.27}} (explained by Scholia as **regular**, τεταγμένη); ἴσος τοῖς παροῦσι {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.132.}}
**III** of ground, **even, flat**, εἰς τὸ ἴ. καταβαίνειν, of an army, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.6.18 (but ἐν ἴσῳ προσιέναι}} to advance **with even step**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 1.8.11)}}; λέουσιν εἰς τὸ ἴ. καθιστάμενοι μάχεσθαι, opposed to μετὰ πλεονεξίας ἀγωνίζεσθαι, **on even terms**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 1.6.28}}; ἴ. τοῖχος, opposed to κεκλικώς, **perpendicular**, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Philoponus, Joannes Philosophus “in Aristotelis Analytica Posteriora commentaria - pars” 2.27.}}
**IV** _adverb_ ἴσως (see. entry): but also,
**IV.1** _neuter_ _singular_ and _plural_ from {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus downwards (see. entry [near the start]), ἶσον.. ἀπήχθετο κηρὶ μελαίνῃ}} **even as** Death, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.454}}; ἶσον ἐμοὶ βασίυε be king **like** me, {{Refs|9.616}}; ἶσον γάρ σε θεῷ τίσουσιν Ἀχαιοί{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 603; ἶσον ἐμῇ κεφαλῇ 18.82; τὸν.. ἶσα θεῷ.. εἰσορόωσιν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.520; ἶσα φίλοισι τέκεσσι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.71, compare 13.176, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.432, 11.304, etc.}}: later _absolutely_, **alike**, δείλαιε τοῦ νοῦ τῆς τε συμφορᾶς ἴσον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1347; τὴν Σάμον καὶ Ἡρακλέας στήλας ἴσον ἀπέχειν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.132: with _dative_, ἴσον ναοῖς θεῶν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 801}}; ἴσον ἄπεσμεν τῷ πρίν **equally** as before, [{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Hipp.” 302 (variant{τῶν πρίν}); ἴσα τοῖς πάνυ 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus “fragment” 70.6}}]; ἴσα καί.. **like as, as if**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1187 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 994 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.14; ἴσον ὡς.. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1363}}; ὥσπερ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 532; ὥστε.. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 882; ἅτε.. [prev. author] “HF” 667 (Lyric poetry); ὅσονπερ.. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 15.1.}}
**IV.2** with Preps.: ἀπὸ τῆς ἴσης **equally**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.15; ὁ ἀπὸ τῆς ἴ. ἐχθρός [prev. author] 3.40; ἀπ᾽ ἴσης εἶναι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 14.6; ἀφ᾽ ἵσου “SIG” 426.14 (Teos, 3rd c.BC)}}; δι᾽ ἴσου {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 43.37}}; at **equal** distance, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 617b}}: also in in Mathematics texts, **ex aequali**, of proportions, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 5 “Def.” 17, +others}}; δι᾽ ἴ. ἐν τεταραγμένῃ ἀναλογίᾳ **ex aequali** in disturbed proportion, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ σφαίρας καὶ κυλίνδρου” 2.4, +others, 3rd c.AD(?): Pappus Mathematicus 932.11}}; ἐν ἴσῳ **equally**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.53, 4.65}}; ἐν ἴσῳ ἐστί it matters not, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1199; ἐν ἴσῳ [ἐστὶ] καὶ εἰ.. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.60; ἐν τῷ ἴσῳ εἶναι [prev. author] 4.10; ἐξ ἴσης 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 861a: more frequently ἐξ ἴσου 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.135, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 563, etc.; ἐξ ἴ. τινί [prev. author] “Ant.” 516, 644, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 517a}}; **evenly**, εὐθεῖα γραμμή ἐστιν ἥτις ἐξ ἴ. τοῖς ἐφ᾽ ἑαυτῆς σημείοις κεῖται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 1 “Def.” 4; ἐξ ἴ. καὶ.. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 254; ὡς.. [prev. author] “OT” 61}}; οἱ ἐξ ἴ. persons **of equal station**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 777d, compare 919d; ὁ ἐξ ἴ. κίνδυνος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.4.4; ἐκ τοῦ ἴ. γίγνεσθαί τινι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.3; τοῖς ἐκ τοῦ ἴσου ἡμῖν οὖσι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 8.5; ἐξ ἴσου τισὶ τῆς πολιτείας μεταδιδόναι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 25.3}}; ἐκ τοῦ ἴ. μάχεσθαι **to be evenly matched**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.16; ἐξ ἴ. πολεμεῖν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.47}}; κατὰ μῆνα τὸ αἱροῦν ἐξ ἴ. the sum due in **equal** monthly instalments, {{Refs|“PAmh.” 2.92.14, etc.}}; ἐπὶ or ἐπ᾽ ἴσης, ἐπὶ ἴ. διαφέρειν τὸν πόλεμον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.74; τοῦτο ἐπ᾽ ἴσης ἔχει [prev. author] 7.50, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1062 (Lyric poetry), etc.; ἐπ᾽ ἴσου 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.18.10; ἐπ᾽ ἴσον [prev. author] 6.38.4, cf. Docum. cited in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.106, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.21 W.; ἐπὶ ἶσα μάχη τέτατο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.436; compare κατὰ ἶσα μάχην ἐτάνυσσε 11.336; κατ᾽ ἴσον 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.68.6, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 1.19}}; μετ᾽ ἴσου **equally**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Demetrius Lacon Philosophus “Herc.” 124.12.}}
**V** _comparative_ ἰσαίτερος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 441, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.89, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.1.14: _superlative_ ἰσότατος Timo 68; ἰσαίτατος 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.462}}. _adverb_ ὡς ἰσαίτατα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 744c, but ὡς ἰσότατα “SIG” 531.30 (from Dyme)}}. [ῑ in early _Epic dialect_ (except {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 752), compare 6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 24.1: ῐ first in 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 678, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 2.1 (but ἶσος Ead.91 assuming variant), 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 5.46 (but ἶσον 1.62, “fragment” 2.2), and always in 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus}} (except in _compound_ ῑσοδαίμων) and Trag. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|(4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 216 is uncertain reading) except in _compound_ ῑσό-θεος (which see)}}; uncertain in ἰσ-όνειρος. Both quantities are found in later poetry, sometimes in same line, ἔχοισαν ἴσον κάτω, ἶσον ἄνωθεν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 8.19; πρέσβυν ἴσον κούροις, ἶσον ἁδόντα κόραις “APl.” 4.309.]}}

2471:
** ἰσότης**, ητος, ἡ,
(ἴσος) **equality**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1054b3, etc.: in _plural_, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 733b; ἰ. χρόνου [prev. author] “Prm.” 140e: in Mathematics texts, ἰ. γεωμετρική}} **equality of ratios, proportion**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Grg.” 508a; ἀναλογία ἰ. λόγων 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1131a31.}}
**2** especially **political equality** or **justice**, personified in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 536, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 757a; ἰ. πολιτική 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.8.4, etc.: in dual, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 757b, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1302a7, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.21.}}
**II** **fair dealing, impartiality**, {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 259, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.38.8, NT.Col.4.1.}}
**III** **equiformity**, of the earth, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 11.10. (On the accent see Hdn.Gr. 1.83, 2.945}}; ἰσοτής is said to be Hellenistic by {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Moeris Grammaticus 202.)}}

2472:
** ἰσότιμ-ος**, ον,
**equal in honour** or **privilege**, Απόλλων (i.e. sharing the honours paid to Zeus) {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“OGI” 234.25 (Delph., 3rd c.BC), “CR Acad.Inscription” 1906.419 (from Alabanda); ὁ θεὸς.. -ον παρέχι τράπεζαν τοῖς ὁποθενοῦν ἀφικνουμένοις “BCH” 51.73 (from Panamara)}}; πίστις {{NT|NT.2Pet.1.1}}; οἱ πρῶτοι καὶ ἰ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lysander” 19, cf. Wilcken “Chr.” 13.10 (1st c.AD), 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “D Mort.” 24.3, etc.; πόλεις τισί 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 41.2}}: _comparative_ -ότεροι, τοῖς κρατοῦσιν {{Refs|[prev. author] 39.4}}; τὸ ἰ.,= ἰσοτιμία, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.246}}; of a person, **maintaining equality of privilege**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 2.4.9}}. _adverb_ -μως, τινάς τισιν ἄγειν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 5.177c}}; ζῶντα δικαίως καὶ ἰ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“OGI” 544.34 (Ancyra, 2nd c.AD),compare “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 4032.5 ([prev. passage]), “IGRom.” 3.195 ([prev. passage]); ἰ. ἔχουσι πρὸς ἀλλήλους οἱ ὅροι 6th c.AD: Philoponus, Joannes Philosophus “in Aristotelis Analytica Priora commentaria - pars” 167.14, compare 3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus “in Aristotelis Metaphysica commentaria - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 241.11.}}
**2** generally, **equal in value**: hence, **equal**, ἁμάρτημα ἀκούσιον ἰ. ἑκουσίῳ {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.248; τὸ ἰ. δυσέφικτον ἐν ταῖς ἀμοιβαῖς 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 2.3.6}}; ἰ. μάχη **evenly balanced**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 10.1.}}
**3** as title of rank at the Ptolemaic court, τῶν ἰσοτίμων τοῖς πρώτοις φίλοις {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PRyl.” 66 _intransitive_, 253 (2nd c.BC), “Arch.Papyrus” 6.372.}}

2473:
** ἰσό-ψῡχος**, ον,
**of equal spirit**, κράτος ἰ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1470 (Lyric poetry)}}. _adverb_ -χως, μάχεσθαι {{Refs|12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 831.52.}}
**2** **of like soul** or **mind,** {{NT|NT.Phil.2.20.}}

2476:
** ἵστημι**
(compare ἱστάω, ἱστάνω),
**I** causal, **make to stand**, _imperative_ ἵστη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.313, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 1230, καθ-ίστα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.202:}}_imperfect_ ἵστην, _Epic dialect_ ἵστασκε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.574}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἵσταν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 10.112:}}_future_ στήσω, _Doric dialect_ στᾱσῶ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.54}}: _aorist 1_ ἔστησα, _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἔστᾰσαν for ἔστησαν uncertain in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.307, 3.182, 8.435, +others (see. ἔστᾰσαν): hence, in late Poets, ἔστᾰσας, ἔστᾰσε, “Anthologia Graeca” 9.714,708 (1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus)}}: _aorist 1_ _middle_ ἐστησάμην (never _intransitive_){{Refs 4th c.BC+|see below 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 111.2, 3}}: _perfect_ ἕστᾰκα {{LXX+5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Cercidas Iambographus 3, (καθ-) 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “pro Euxenippo” 28, “UPZ” 112.5 (2nd c.BC), (περι-) 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 370d, (ἀφ-) LXX.Jer.16.5, (παρ-) 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.9S., +others, (συν-) 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 7.109}}; also ἕστηκα (see. below) in _transitive_ sense, (δι-) {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Ventis” 973a18, (ἀφ-) variant in LXX}} [same place]; ἑστακεῖα _transitive_ in {{Refs|“Test.Epict.” 1.25.}}
**II** _intransitive_, **stand**,
**II.1** _active_, _aorist 2_ ἔστην, _Epic dialect_ στάσκον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.217}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἔστησαν, more frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus ἔσταν, στάν [ᾰ]}}; _imperative_ στῆθι, _Doric dialect_ στᾶθι {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 29, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 23.38}}_subjunctive_ στῶ, _Epic dialect_ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|2 and _3rd.pers._ _sing._ στήῃς, στήῃ (for στῇς, στῇ), 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.30, 5.598}}; _1st pers._ _plural_ στέωμεν (as _disyllable_){{Refs|22.231, στείομεν 15.297}}; _optative_ σταῖεν, _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ σταίησαν {{Refs|17.733}}; _infinitive_ στῆναι, _Epic dialect_ στήμεναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|17.167, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.414}}, _Doric dialect_ στᾶμεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.2}}; _participle_ στάς: _perfect_ ἕστηκα: _pluperfect_ ἑστήκειν, sometimes with strengthened augment εἱστήκειν, as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 925, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 513, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.89, etc.}}; _Ionic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἑστήκεε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.152:—from 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus downwards the shorter dual and _plural_ forms of the}}_perfect_ are preferred, ἕστᾰτον, ἕστᾰμεν, ἕστᾰτε, ἑστᾶσι {{Refs 6th c.BC+|(“IG” 12(8).356 (Thasos, 6th c.BC), etc.), in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus ἑστέᾱσι}}; _imperative_ ἕστᾰθι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristomenes Comicus 5}}; _subjunctive_ ἑστ; _optative_ ἑσταίη; _infinitive_ ἑστάναι, _Epic dialect_ ἑστάμεν, ἑστάμεναι (ἑστηκέναι only late, as {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 3.18)}}; _participle_ ἑστώς (ἑστηκώς rare in early Gr., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.126, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 93d, “Lg.” 802c, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus (below 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 11.2), 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 126.16, εἱστηκότα “IG” 12.374.179), _feminine_ ἑστῶσα (not ἑστυῖα}}; but συνεστηκυιῶν probably in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 10), _neuter_ ἑστός 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 40b, “Tht.” 183e, “SIG” 1234 (from Lycia), etc., (καθ-) “POxy.” 68.32 (2nd c.AD), (ἐν-) “PRyl.” 98 (a). 10 (2nd c.AD), (παρ-) 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 564 (-ώς}} frequently variant as in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus and 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus [same places], preferred by 4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus “Scholia in Theodosii Canones” 2.313)}}; _genitive_ ἑστῶτο; _Ionic dialect_ ἑστεώς, ἑστεός, ῶτο; _Epic dialect_ ἑστηώς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 747}}; _dative_ _plural_ ἑστηῶσι conjecture in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Antimachus Colophonius Elegiacus et Epicus 16.5, compare 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Dianam” 134; 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus does not use the _nominative_, but has _genitive_ ἑστᾰότος, _accusative_ ἑστᾰότα, _nominative_ _plural_ ἑστᾰότες, as if from ἑσταώς: so also}}_pluperfect_ ἑστάτην, ἕστᾰμεν, ἕστᾰτε, ἕστᾰσαν: late _present_ ἑστήκω, formed from _perfect_, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Posidippus Comicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 10.412e}}: hence, _future_ ἑστήξω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus “Epigrammata” 15.14, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.2.17, Hegesippus Epigrammaticus 1.25, ἑστήξομαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 10.9 codices}}
**II.2** _passive_, ἵσταμαι: _imperative_ ἵστασο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 449, ἵστω 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 893, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 737:}}_imperfect_ ἱστάμην: _future_ στᾰθήσομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.34, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.103: more frequently στήσομαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.90, etc.}}: _aorist_ ἐστάθην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.463, etc.}}; rarely ἔστην, _Doric dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἔσστα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 56.43 (Argos, 5th c.BC):}}_perfect_ ἕσταμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(δι-) variant in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 81d, κατεστέαται variant in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.196. (From I.- 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **sthā-**, cf. Sanskrit _sthā-_ (_aorist_ **á-sthā-t**), Latin _stare_, etc.; Gr. _reduplicate_ _present_ and _perfect_ from **si-sthā-, se-sthā-.**)
**A** Causal, **make to stand, set up**, πελέκεας ἑξείης {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.574}}; ἔγχος μέν ῥ᾽ ἔστησε φέρων πρὸς κίονα **he set** it against the pillar,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|1.127, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.126}}; ἱ. ἱστόν **set up** the loom, or **raise** the mast (see. ἱστός {{Refs|1 and 11)}}; κρητῆρας στήσασθαι **to have** bowls **set up**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.431}}; θεοῖς.. κρητῆρα στήσασθαι in honour of the gods, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.528}}; στῆσαί τινα ὀρθόν, στ. ὀρθὰν καρδίαν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.53, 96; ὀρθῷ στ. ἐπὶ σφυρῷ [prev. author] “I.” 7(6).13; ἐς ὀρθὸν ἱ. τινά 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 1230; ὁ Ξανθίας τὸν φαλλὸν ὀρθὸν στησάτω 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 243; ὀρθὸν οὖς ἵστησιν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 27}}; στῆσαι λόγχας, for battle, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ant.” 145 (Lyric poetry)}}; especially **raise** buildings, statues, trophies, etc., ἱ. ἀνδριάντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.110; τροπαῖα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1102; τροπαῖον ἱ. τῶν πολεμίων 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.150, compare “IG” 22.1457.26; τροπαῖον στησάμενοι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.7; τροπαῖον ἂν στήσαιτο τῶν ταύτης τρόπων 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 453; τὰ μακρὰ στῆσαι τείχη 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.69}}; ἱ. τινὰ χαλκοῦν **set** him **up** in brass, **raise** a brazen statue to him, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 13.21, 19.261}} (so in _perfect_, **stand**, οὗτος ἕστηκε λίθινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.141}}:—_passive_, σφυρήλατος ἐν Ὀλυμπία στάθητι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 236b; σταθῆναι χαλκοῦς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1410a33).}}
**A.II** **set, place**, of things or persons, τρίποδ᾽ ἔστασαν ἐν πυρί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.435, etc.; ὥς σ᾽ ἄγχι γῆς στήσωσι Καδμείας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 399, etc.}}; **fix**, τοὺς ὀφθαλμοὺς εἰς τὴν γῆν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 1.10}}; especially **set** men **in order** or **array**, πεζοὺς δ᾽ ἐξόπιθε στῆσεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.298, compare 2.525, etc.; στῆσαί τινας τελευταίους 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.3.25, etc.}}
**A.III** **bring to a standstill, stay, check**, λαὸν δὲ στῆσον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.433}}; νέας, ἵππους, ἡμιόνους στῆσαι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.182, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.755, 24.350}}; μύλην στῆσαι **to stop** the mill, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.111; στῆσεν ἄρ᾽ (i.e. ἡμιόνους) 7.4}}; στῆσε δ᾽ ἐν Ἀμνισῷ (i.e. νῆα){{Refs 4th c.AD+|19.188; βᾶριν 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de Mysteriis” 6.5}}; στῆσαι τὴν φάλαγγα **halt** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.1.5; ἵστησι ῥοῦν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 437b, etc.; ἵ. τὴν ψυχὴν ἐπὶ τοῖς πράγμασιν [prev. work] 437a}}; στ. τὰ ὄμματα **fix** them, of a dying man, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phd.” 118}}; στ. τὸ πρόσωπον **compose** the countenance, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.9; στήσαντες ἐπὶ τούτων τὴν διήγησιν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.2.6: especially in in Medicine texts, ἵ. κοιλίαν 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.20}}; τὰς κοιλίας Philotim. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 4.10.1; αἱμορραγίας 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.129: _absolutely_, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 605a29}}:—_middle_, ἱστάμενος τῷ νοσήματι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιστολαί” 19 (“Hermes” 53.65).}}
**A.III.2** **set on foot, stir up**, κονίης.. ἱστᾶσιν ὀμίχλην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.336; ἵστη δὲ μέγα κῦμα 21.313; νεφέλην ἔστησε Κρονίων 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.405, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.523}}; of battle, etc., φυλόπιδα στήσειν **stir up** strife, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.314; ἔριν στήσαντες 16.292 (so _intransitive_ φύλοπις ἕστηκε}} the fray **is on foot**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.172):}} —also in _middle_, στησάμενοι δ᾽ ἐμάχοντο{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 533, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.54; πολέμους ἵστασθαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.9.β᾽, 175, 236; so ἱστάναι βοήν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 885; κραυγήν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1529 (_passive_, θόρυβος ἵσταται βοῆς}} **arises**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1263)}}; also of passions and states of mind, μῆνιν, ἐλπίδα στῆσαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “OT” 699, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 788 (Lyric poetry).}}
**A.III.3** **set up, appoint**, τινὰ βασιλέα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.97; τύραννον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 940, compare “OC” 1041, “Ant.” 666}}:—_middle_, ἐστάσαντο τύραννον {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 37 A; φύλακας στησόμεθα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 484d}}:—_passive_, ὁ ὑπὸ Δαρείου σταθεὶς ὕπαρχος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.105, compare “IG” 9(1).32.23 (Stiris, 2nd c.BC).}}
**A.III.4** **establish, institute**, χορούς, παννυχίδα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.48, 4.76 (so στήσασθαι ἤθεά τε καὶ νόμους [prev. author] 2.35; ἀγῶνα “hymnus ad Apollinem” 150)}}; στῆσαι χορόν, Ὀλυμπιάδα, ἑορτάν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.114, “O.” 2.3, 10(11).58; κτερίσματα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 433; χορούς 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 10.112, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.51}}; οὐχ ὑγιῶς ἱστάμενος λόγον **setting up** a bad argument, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Anonymus Londinensis 26.34}}:—_passive_, ἀγορὴ ἵσταταί τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.58.}}
**A.III.5** = Latin _statuere_, **determine**, γνῶναι καὶ στῆσαι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 8.68; διαγεινώσκειν καὶ ἱστάναι “Not. Arch.” 4.21 (Aug.)}}:—_passive_, τὰ ὑπό τινος σταθέντα {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“OGI” 665.27 (Egypt, 1st c.AD)}}; τὰ ἑσταμένα Wilcken {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Chr.” 167.27 (2nd c.BC).}}
**A.III.6** **fix by agreement**, ὁ σταθεὶς τόκος {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“PGrenf.” 1.31.1 (1st c.BC),compare “PFlor.” 14.11 (4th c.AD)}}; τὸ ἑσταμένον ἐνοίκιον {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“BGU” 253.15 (3rd c.AD).}}
**A.III.7** **bring about, cause**, ἀμπνοάν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.199}}; στῆσαι δύσκηλον χθόνα **make** its case desperate, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 825.}}
**A.IV** **place in the balance, weigh**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.247, 22.350, 24.232, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 40}}; [ἐκπώματα] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 18.7; ἀριθμοῦντες καὶ μετροῦντες καὶ ἱστάντες 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.2.21, etc.}}; ἱστάναι τι πρὸς ἀργύριον **weigh** a thing against silver, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.65}}; ἀγαθὸς ἱστάναι good **at weighing**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 356b}}; τὸ ἐγγὺς καὶ τὸ πόρρω στήσας ἐν τῷ ζυγῷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. passage],compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 10.18}}; ἐπὶ τὸ ἱστάναι ἐλθεῖν have recourse to **the scales**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 7c}}:—_passive_, ἵστασθαι ἐπὶ ζυγοῦ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.29.15; σταθείς}}**weighed,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 11(2).161 B 113 (Delos, 3rd c.BC).}}
**A.IV.2** **weigh out, pay**, {{LXX+NT|LXX.1Ki.21.39, cf. LXX.Zech.11.12, NT.Matt.26.15.}}
**B** _passive_ and _intransitive_ tenses of _active_, **to be set** or **placed, stand**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus etc., ἀγχοῦ, ἆσσον, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.172, 23.97; ἄντα τινός 17.30; ἐς μέσσον 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.447; σταθεὶς ἐς μέσον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.130}}; ἀντίοι ἔσταν, ἐναντίοι ἔστησαν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.535, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.391}}: proverbial of critical circumstances, ἐπὶ ξυροῦ ἵσταται ἀκμῆς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.173: frequently merely a stronger form of εἶναι}}, **to be** in a certain place or state, ἀργύρεοι σταθμοὶ ἐν χαλκέῳ ἕστασαν οὐδῷ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.89, etc.}}; ἑστάτω for ἔστω, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1084}}; τὰ νῦν ἑστῶτα,= τὰ νῦν, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tr.” 1271 (_anapaest meter_); ἐμοὶ δ᾽ ἄχος ἕστᾱκεν [prev. author] “Aj.” 200 (Lyric poetry): with}} _adverb_, ξυμφορᾶς ἵν᾽ ἕσταμεν, ἵν᾽ ἕστ. χρείας, in what case or need **we are**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tr.” 1145, “OT” 1442}}; ποῦ τύχης ἕστηκε; {{Refs|[prev. author] “Aj.” 102}}; later also ἀδίκως, ὀρθῶς, εὐλαβῶς ἵστασθαι, **behave** wrongly, etc., {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.3.2, 33.6.3, 18.33.4.}}
**B.2** **take up an** intellectual **attitude**, ὡς ἵστασθαι δεῖ περὶ χρημάτων κτήσεως {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ οἰκονομίας” p.38J.}}; οὐκ ὀρθῶς ἵ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Rh.” 1.53S.}}
**B.3** in pregnant sense, στῆναι ἐς.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.21; στ. ἐς δίκην 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 962; στ. παρά τινα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.169 (but οἱ μὴ στάντες παρὰ τὰ δεινά}} those who did not **face** the danger, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 9.28): with _accusative_ loci, τί τοῦτ᾽ αἰθερίαν ἕστηκε πέτρα; 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 987 (Lyric poetry); στῆτε τόνδε τρίβον [prev. author] “Or.” 1251:with _accusative_ _cognate_, ποίαν μ᾽ ἀνάστασιν δοκεῖς.. στῆνα; 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 277.}}
**B.II** **stand still, halt**, ἀλλ᾽ ἄγε δὴ στέωμεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.348, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.211, 10.97; opposed to φεύγω, 6.199}}, etc.; **stand idle**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.243, +others}}; ἑστάναι **to be stationary**, opposed to κινεῖσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 436c, etc.; κατὰ χώρην ἑστάναι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.97}}; οὐ μὴν ἐνταῦθ᾽ ἕστηκε τὸ πρᾶγμα **does** not **rest** here, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.102, compare 10.36}}; ἐὰν ἡ κοιλία στῇ if the bowels **are constipated**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 588a8}}: with _participle_, οὐ στήσεται ἀδικῶν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 10.10}}; **come to a stop, rest satisfied**, ἄν τις ὀρθῶς ἐπιβάλῃ, ἔπειτα σταθῇ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 423; οὐχ ἱστάμενοι 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 3.1.2: _impersonal_, ἵσταται}} **there is a stop, one comes to a stop**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Priora” 43a37, +others; οὐκ ἔστη ἐνταῦθα κακοῖς γενομένοις ἀποθανεῖν 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 3.2.8; also ἵστασθαι μέχρι τοῦ γένους 4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “Aristotelis Analyticorum Posteriorum paraphrasis - pars” 55.8, +others}}
**B.II.2** _metaphorically_, **stand firm**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.2.23; τῇ διανοίᾳ 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 21.11.3}}; of arguments or propositions, **hold good**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.83, 2.192 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus:}}_participle_, ἑστηκώς **fixed, stable**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 776a35, “EN” 1104a4, “Metaphorical” 1047a15; δεῖ τὸ κρίμα ἑστηκὸς καὶ κύριον εἶναι “SIG” 826ii29 (Delph., 2nd c.BC)}}; λογισμὸς ἑστὼς καὶ νουνεχής {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.105.9; τέχναι οὐκ ἔχουσαι τὸ ἑστηκός, ἀλλὰ τὸ στοχαστικόν 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.71S. (so}} _adverb_ ἑστηκότως, opposed to στοχαστικῶς{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. work]70S.),compare 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “Protrepticus” 21. κ; χρεία ἑστηκυῖα καὶ τεταγμένη 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.25.10}}; ἑστηκότα θεωρήματα, ἑστηκότες σκοποί, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.2S., “Po.” 5.22}}; of age, ἑστηκυῖα ἡλικία {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 802c}}; τιμαὶ ἑστηκυῖαι **fixed** prices, **PTeb.unpublished**{{Refs|703.177.}}
**B.III** **to be set up** or **upright, stand up, rise up**, κρημνοὶ ἕστασαν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.55; ὀρθαὶ τρίχες ἔσταν 24.359, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 564 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Ion” 535c, etc.; κονίη ἵστατο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.151; ἵστατο κῦμα 21.240}}; of a horse, ἵστασθαι ὀρθός to **rear**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.111}}; ἵστασθαι βάθρων **from** the steps, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 143.}}
**B.III.2** **to be set up, erected**, or **built**, στήλη, ἥ τ᾽ ἐπὶ τύμβῳ ἑστήκῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.435; ἕστακε τροπαῖον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 954 (Lyric poetry); μνημεῖον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 268, etc.}}; see above {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11.}}
**B.III.3** generally, **arise, begin**, ἵστατο νεῖκος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.333; compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 111.2.}}
**B.III.4** in marking Time, ἔαρος νέον ἱσταμένοιο when spring **is** not long **begun**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.519}}; ἕβδομος ἑστήκει μείς the seventh month **was begun**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.117}}; τοῦ μὲν φθίνοντος μηνός, τοῦ δ᾽ ἱσταμένοιο as one month ends and the next **begins**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.162, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 780}}; later μὴν ἱστάμενος, μεσῶν, φθίνων, first in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.57, 106, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.121, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.67; σχεδὸν ἤδη μεσημβρία ἵσταται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 242a.}}
**B.III.5** **to be appointed**, στῆναι ἐς ἀρχήν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.80}}; see above {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 111.3.}}

2477:
** ἱστορ-έω**,
(ἵστωρ) **inquire into** or **about** a thing, τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.113, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 632, etc.; περί τινος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.48.12}}; also, **inquire about** a person, τινα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1150, 1156; ὅδ᾽ εἴμ᾽ Ὀρέστης.. ὃν ἱστορεῖς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 380, compare “Tr.” 261: followed by _relative_ clause, Αἴγισθον ἔνθ᾽ ᾤκηκεν ἱστορῶ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1101.}}
**2** **examine, observe**, χώραν, πόλιν, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Theseus” 30, “Pomp.” 40, compare 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 1.11.4}}; τὴν τοῦ Μέμνονος [σύριγγα] {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“OGI” 694.7; τὴν σύνεσίν τινος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cicero” 2, etc.; τινὰς ἀπολουμένους 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 11.109}}: hence, **to be informed about, know**, κακῶς τὸ μέλλον ἱστορῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 454; πατέρα ἱστορεῖς καλῶς [prev. author] “Eu.” 455, compare 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “παραγγελίαι” 12: _metaphorically_, εἴ τις ἀκτὶς ἡλίου νιν ἱστορεῖ βλέποντα}} **has news of** him, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 676: followed by _relative_, τὴν πορείαν ἱστορῶν, ὡς δυσδίοδος ὑπάρχει 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.61.3}}; **read in history**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.63.7.}}
**2** with _accusative_ _person_, **inquire of, ask**, ἱστορέων αὐτοὺς ἥντινα δύναμιν ἔχει ὁ Νεῖλος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.19, compare 3.77}}; **inquire of an oracle**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1547}}; **visit** a person for the purpose of inquiry, Κηφᾶν {{NT|NT.Gal.1.18}}:— _passive_, **to be questioned**, κληθέντας ἱστορέεσθαι εἰ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.24; ἱστορούμενος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 415, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1371.}}
**2.b** with _doubleaccusative_, **inquire of** one **about** a thing, τί μ᾽ ἱστορεῖς τόδε {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 621, compare 3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 1.}}
**4** _absolutely_, **inquire**, ἀκοῆ ἱ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.29, etc.}}; especially in _participle_, ἱστορέων εὕρισκε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.56, etc.; οὔθ᾽ ὁρῶν οὔθ᾽ ἱστορῶν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1484}}; followed by a _relative_ word, ἱστόρεόν τε ὅτεῳ τρόπῳ περιγένοιτο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.122.}}
**II** **give an account of what one has learnt, record**, τοὺς βίους τῶν χερσαίων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.13.1, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 7, etc.}}; ἱστοροῦσί τινες.. **it is stated that.. **, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.75, etc.}}:—frequently in _passive_, ὁ καρπὸς.. ἐπιλημπτικοὺς ἱστορεῖται ὠφελεῖν {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.83; περί τινος ἱστορεῖται διότι 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Mus.” p.18K.; ἱστορεῖται περὶ Γοργοῦς τοιοῦτον 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.227e, compare [prev. author] “Cic.” 1, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Tactica - Griechische Kriegsschriftsteller” 34.3, etc.}}; Ἀπολλόδωρος εἴρηκεν ἀπελθόντας Ὕαντας ἱστορεῖσθαι **are represented** as having gone, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 10.3.4}}; τῶν ἱστορουμένων οὐδενὸς ἧττον πολυπράγμων the most in dustrious person **on record**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Mus.” p.108K.}}

2478:
** ἰσχῡρός**, ά, όν,
(ἰσχύς) **strong**, especially of personal strength, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 945, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 290, etc.}}; of things, ἰ. βέλος {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 15.4; ῥεύματα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.12}}; ἰ. χθών **hard**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 310}}; of food, **indigestible**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 50}}; of taste, **strong**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 7.6.1}}; of armies, ἰσχυροτέρα φάλαγξ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.1.30}}; of places, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.9, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.6.11, etc.; τῆς χώρης τὸ -ότατον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.76}}; τὸ ἑαυτοῦ ἰ., opposed to τὰ τοῖς πολεμίοις ἰ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitum magistro” 8.24}}; τὰ ἰσχυρότατα your **strongest points**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.111}}; τὰ τῆς πόλεως ἰ. **that in which the strength** of the state **lies**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.66}}; ὁρῶντες οὐδὲν ἰ. ἀπὸ τῶν Λεσβίων no **show of strength**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.6}}; ἰ. τι πρὸς τὸ πρᾶγμ᾽ ἔχειν a **strong** point, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 130; -ότατον τεκμήριον “SIG” 685.84 (Crete, 2nd c.BC).}}
**2** **powerful**, ἄλοχος Διός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 302; πόλις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 447; θεός 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 946; ἰ. τὸ πολλόν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.136; οἱ ἰ. ἐν ταῖς πόλεσιν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Atheniensium” 1.14}}: _comparative_ -ότερος, ἐς πειθώ {{NT+3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 51; ὁ ὀπίσω μου ἐρχόμενος -ότερός μου ἐστίν NT.Matt.3.11.}}
**3** **forcible, violent, severe**, σιτοδείη, ψύχη, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.94, 4.29; λιμός NT.Luke.15.14; ἀναγκαίη 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.74}}; αἱ λίαν ἰ. τιμωρίαι **violent, excessive**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.205}}; ὅρκος -ότατος, ἀνάγκαι -όταται, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.11, 6.25; νόσημα 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Acut.(Sp.”) 4; βήξ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.49}}; γέλως, ἐπιθυμίαι, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 388e, 560b, etc.}}; νόμος ἰ. **severe**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.102, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 15.9; ἔχθρα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 233c}}; γνώμη -οτέρη **more positive**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.41}}; τρόπῳ ᾧ ἂν δύνωνται -οτάτῳ Foed. cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.23}}; κατὰ τὸ ἰσχυρόν **by main force**, opposed to δόλῳ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.201, compare 9.2.}}
**4** of literary style, **vigorous**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 22}}; also of syllables, **strong**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 16; στάσεις λαμβάνειν ἰ. [prev. work] 22.}}
**II** _adverb_ -ρῶς **strongly, with all force**, ἐγκεῖσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.69, etc.; φυλάττειν τινάς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.3.11.}}
**II.2** **very much, exceedingly**, with _adjectives_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.108}}; ἔθνος μέγα ἰ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|. [prev. work] 183; διώρυγες ἰ. βαθεῖαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” [1.7.15], etc.; ἰ. χλωρόν 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προγνωστικόν” 11}}; κίνησις νωθὴς ἰ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 503b9; ἰ. φιλοπλάτων 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ind Sto.” 61: with Verbs, ἰ. ἥδεσθαι, ἀνιᾶσθαι, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.44; ἀπήγγειλεν ὅτι πάντα δοκοίη ἰ. τῷ εὐνούχῳ [prev. work] 5.3.15}}: _comparative_ **-οτέρως**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 114, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.129}}; **-ότερον** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.12, etc.: _superlative_, in answers, ἰσχυρότατά γε}} **most certainly**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Oec.” 1.15.}}

2479:
** ἰσχ-ύς**
[see. entry near the end], ύος, ἡ, **strength** of body, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 146, 823, etc.; ἀκμαὶ ἰσχύος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.96; δεινὸν ἰσχύος θράσος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 104; τὴν ἰ. δεινὰ καὶ τὴν ῥώμην 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 190b; πρὸς ἰσχὺν ὀφθαλμοὶ ἄριστα πεφυκότες 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 5.5: _plural_, ἰσχύες καὶ ἀσθένειαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 618d; κατὰ σωμάτων ἰσχῦς καὶ εὐμορφίας [prev. author] “Lg.” 744c}}; of places, ἰσχὺς γῆς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 610}}; of a fortified place, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.35.}}
**2** **might, power**, θεοῦ, θεῶν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 226 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 118; ἰ. βασιλεία 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 590 (Lyric poetry), compare 12 (_anapaest meter_)}}; ὅπου γὰρ ἰ. συζυγοῦσι καὶ δίκη **might** and right, {{Refs|[prev. author] “fragment” 381}}; φύσεως ἰ., of Themistocles, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.138}}; ἐπὶ μέγα ἐλθεῖν ἰσχύος to a great height **of power**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.97, compare 1.85, etc.; παρὰ ἰσχὺν τῆς δυνάμεως [prev. author] 7.66}}; ἰ. μάχης fighting **power**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.97; ἰ. τῆς ἐλπίδος [prev. author] 4.65, compare 2.62}}; ἡ τῶν νόμων ἰ. {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“POxy.” 67.14 (4th c.AD)}}; **validity,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|PGrenf. 2.71ii11 (3rd c.AD), etc.}}
**3** **brute force**, κατ᾽ ἰσχύν **perforce**, opposed to δόλῳ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 214; πρὸς ἰσχύος κράτος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 594; πρὸς ἰσχύος χάριν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 538; ὑπὸ τῆς ἰσχύος 4th c.BC: Epicrates Comicus 3.10; κατέχοντες ἰσχύϊ τὸ πλῆθος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.62; εἴ τι ἰσχύϊ πράττεται, ἰσχυρῶς πράττεται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 332b.}}
**4** **motive force**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 250a6}}; ἡ κινοῦσα ἰ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cael.” 275b20, +others}}
**5** in Lit. Crit., **vigour** of style, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Epistula ad Pompeium” 3, “Comp.” 2, +others}}
**II** in Tactics, the **main body** of troops, οὔπω ἡ ἰ. πάρεστιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.19}}. [ῠ in _genitive_, etc.: in _nominative_ and _accusative_ _singular_ ῠ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 11.31 (_accusative_)}}: ῡ in Trag. and Comedy texts, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 1080 (_anapaest meter_), “Ch.” 721 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 118, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 449.] (Perh. ϝισχύς, compare βίσχυν, γισχύν.)}}

2480:
** ἰσχ-ύω**
(ἰσχύς), {{Refs|Batrachomyomachia 279}}: _imperfect_ ἴσχυον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 357, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.4.18}}: _future_ ἰσχύσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1607, etc.}}: _aorist_ ἴσχῡσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 502, etc.}}: _perfect_ ἴσχῡκα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.165, 3rd c.BC: Cercidas Iambographus 17.34}}:—_passive_, _aorist_ κατ-ισχύθην {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 15.87}}: (ἰσχύς):—**to be strong** in body, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 234; ὅπως ὑγιαίνοιεν καὶ ἰσχύοιεν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.24; ὃς μέγιστον ἴσχυσε στρατοῦ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 502; ἰ. τοῖς σώμασιν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.7.7; τοῦ σώματος ἰσχύοντος 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.93}}; ἴσχυόν τ᾽ αὐτὸς ἐμαυτοῦ, i.e. I **had** all my **strength**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 357}}; ἰ. ἐκ νόσου **to be recovering**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.4.18.}}
**2** **to be powerful, prevail**, μηδὲν μεῖον ἰ. Διός {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 510, etc.}}; πλέον, μεῖζον ἰ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1188, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1607}}; later ἰ. πρός τινα **prevail** against, {{LXX|LXX.Psa.12(13).4}}; ἐπί τινας{{LXX|LXX.1Mac.10.49}}; ἰ. τινί **to be strong in** a thing, σοφία ἀνὴρ ὑπὲρ ἀνδρὸς ἰσχύει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 61; θράσει 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 903; ἐν τῇ ποιητικῇ 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ ποιημάτων” 5.9; ἰ. τινὶ πρὸς τοὺς πολεμίους 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.46; ἰ. ἐκ πονηρίας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.9}}; ὅθεν ἰσχύομεν, ᾗπερ ἰσχύουσι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.143, 2.13}}; ἰ. παρά τινι **have power** or **influence** with one, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 8.47, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.2, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 38.20, etc.}}
**2.b** of things, **prevail**, ὅρκος οὔτι Ζηνὸς ἰ. πλέον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 621; τἀληθὲς γὰρ ἰσχῦον τρέφω 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 356}}; τὸ δίκαιον ἐν πᾶσιν ἰ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 37.59}}; **have force**, ἃ ὡρίσω σὺ δίκαια, ταὐτὰ.. καὶ κατὰ σοῦ προσήκει τοῖς ἄλλοις ἰσχύειν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.241, compare 25.71; ὁ λόγος δόξειεν ἂν ἰσχύειν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1280a28}}; νομὴ ἄδικος οὐδὲν ἰ. **is of** no **force,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|PTeb. 286.7 (2nd c.AD)}}; ἰσχῦόν τι something **permanent**, probably in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.7U.}}: c._infinitive_, ὁ καιρὸς ἰσχύει.. πράττειν {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 17.9, cf. LXX.2Ch.2.6(5), +others, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 58; οὐκ ἰ. ἀρτιστομεῖν 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 14.2.28, cf. NT.Mark.5.4, 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 33.22, etc.}}
**3** **to be worth** or **equivalent to**, ἡ μνᾶ ἰσχύει λίτρας δύο καὶ ἥμισυ {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 14.7.1, compare “PGnom.” 106, 2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 134}}; αἱ ψῆφοι τάλαντον ἰσχύουσιν (probably to be read instead of ἴσχουσιν) {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.26.13.}}
**4** _active_, **condense**, νεφέλας {{LXX|LXX.Sir.43.15.}}
**4.b** ἄρτον πᾶσαν ἡδονὴν ἰσχύοντα **making strong.. **,{{LXX|LXX.Wis.16.20}} (**in se habentem**, Vulg.).
**5** -ύοντες ἀστέρες those **in dominating positions**, Serapion in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 8(4).226. [ῡ in Batrachomyomachia [same place], Trag. and Comedy texts, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1409, “OT” 356, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 357, “Av.” 488, 1606}}; later, ῠ sometimes in _present_ and _imperfect_, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 5.166 (3rd c.BC: Asclepiades Epigrammaticus), 211 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus).]}}

2481:
** ἴσως**,
_Laconian dialect_ **ϝίὡρ** (see. βίωρ), _adverb_ of ἴσος, **equally, in like manner,** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 25.11, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 758, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 805a, etc.; ὡς ἰσαίτατα [prev. work] 744c}}; **evenly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “κατ᾽ ἰητρεῖον” 3.}}
**II** **equally, with reference to equality,** τὸ ὀρθὸν ληπτέον ἴσως {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1283b40}}; **fairly, equitably,** ἴ. καὶ κοινῶς Aen.{{Refs 4th c.BC+|Tact. 22.24; οὐκ ἴ. οὐδὲ πολιτικῶς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 10.74; μηδὲν ἴ. καὶ δικαίως φρονοῦντας 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 10.40; οὐκ ἴσως χρήσασθαί τινι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 23.2.7.}}
**III** **probably, perhaps,** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 33, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.124, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 319, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 144, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 473b, etc.; ἴ. που 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 518; οὔτε συμφόρως οὔτ᾽ ἴ. καλῶς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 5.10; οὐκ ἴσως, ἀλλ᾽ ὄντως 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 965c: ironical, σμικρά γε ἴ. προσθήκη [prev. author] “R.” 339b: frequently joined with ἄν or τάχ᾽ ἄν, e.g. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 691, 1009, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 31a; ἀμφισβητοῦντες προστιθέασιν ἀεὶ τὸ ἴσως καὶ τάχα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1389b18: ἴσως without ἄν}} with _optative_ is _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 727, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1055}}; ἴ. μέν.., ἴ. δέ.. **perhaps** so or so, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.3.2: repeated ἴ., ἴ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1320, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.33}}: used to soften or qualify a positive assertion, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 661, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 224, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 61c, “Phdr.” 233e, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 987a26, etc.}}
**IV** with numerals, **about,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1058, 4th-3rd c.BC: Damoxenus Comicus 3.2.}}

2486:
** ἰχθῦς**
(so Hdn.Gr.2.936, -ῦς and -ύς frequently in codices), ύος, ὁ, _accusative_ ἰχθύν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 306, cf. Hdn.Gr. 1.416, 4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus “Scholia in Theodosii Canones” 1.383}}, in late Poets also ἰχθύα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.227 (from Bianor), 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 21.45: _vocative_ ἰχθύ Erinna Lyrica 1, 5th c.BC: Crates Comicus 14: _plural_ ἰχθύες 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 109e, etc., ἰχθῦς 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 261.9, _accusative_ ἰχθύας}}, _contraction_ ἰχθῦς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.53}}, both forms being used in Comedy texts, **-ῦς**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1068, 5th-6th c.BC: Archippus Comicus 29}}, **-ύας** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 68.12, 4th c.BC: Ephippus Comicus 21; codices vary in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 564b19, “PA” 644a21, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 5.3, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 8.3.19, etc.}} **-ῦς** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“SIG” 997.1 (Smyrna, perhaps 1st c.BC)}}; **-ύας**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|“BGU” 1123.9 (Aug.), etc.: dual ἰχθῦ 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 194.15}}:—**fish**, ὠμηστής {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.82, compare 21.122, +others, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 277, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.93, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1297,etc.: proverbial, ἀφωνότερος τῶν ἰχθύων 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Gall.” 1, compare 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 2.18}}: _metaphorically_, of **a stupid fellow**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.975b.}}
**II** in _plural_, οἱ ἰχθύες **the fish-market** at Athens, παρὰ τοὺς ἰχθῦς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1068; ἐν τοῖς ἰχθύσι [prev. author] “V.” 789, 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 125.1.}}
**III** _plural_, the constellation {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Pisces”, 4th c.BC: Eudoxus Astronomus cited in 2nd c.BC: Hipparchus Astronomicus 1.2.3; ἰ. Διός 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Antro Nympharum - Porphyrii Opuscula” 22. (ἱχθύς}} _Attic dialect_ _accusative_ to {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Gellius, Aulus Grammaticus Latinus 2.3.2.)}} [ῡ in _disyllable_ cases, _nominative_ **-ῡς**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.127, 4th-3rd c.BC: Damoxenus Comicus 2.20, 4th c.BC: Archestratus Epicus “fragment” 52 (ἰχθῦς οὖσα should be read for ἰχθὺς ἐοῦσα in 4th c.BC: Matro Parodius “Convivium - Corpusculum poesis epicae ludibundae” 35}}), _accusative_ **-ῡν** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 120, 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 166.7, 4th c.BC: Archestratus Epicus “fragment” 28}}, but **-ῠν** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 21.49, and apparently 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus}} [same place]: ῠ in _trisyllable_ cases and in all compounds] (Cf. Lithuanian _žuvìs_, Armenian _jukn_, 'fish'.)

2487:
** ἴχνος**, εος, τό,
**track, footstep**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.317, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 680, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.82}}; of the **spoor** of game, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 6.15, etc.}}: _metaphorically_, **track, trace**, κατ᾽ ἴχνος πλατᾶν ἄφαντον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 695 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἐς ταὐτὸν ἐλθὼν.. λόγων ἴ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pr.” 845; ἴ. κακῶν ῥινηλατούσῃ [prev. author] “Ag.” 1184; ἴ. παλαιᾶς δυστέκμαρτον αἰτίας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 109; ἴ. τειχέων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 108; ἴχνη τῶν πληγῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 524c; τὰ τῶν κονδύλων ἴ. 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.212}}: with _negative_, not **a trace**, μαζῶν οὐδὲ ἴχνη {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων χρονίων παθῶν” 1.8}}; ἴ. ποδὸς θεῖναι, Latin _vestigia ponere_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 752, compare “Or.” 234; θέσθαι “Anthologia Graeca” 7.464 (Antip.)}}; λεπτὸν ἴ. ἀρβύλης τίθετε step softly, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 140 (Lyric poetry); ἴ. ἐπαντέλλειν ποδός [prev. author] “Ph.” 105 (Lyric poetry); ἴ. ἐρείδειν “Anthologia Graeca” 5.300 (Paul. Sil.)}}; ἐν ἴχνεσί τινος πόδα νέμειν (_metaphorically_) {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 6.15; ἰχνῶν τινος ἔχεσθαι 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 64.4; τοῖς στοιχοῦσι τοῖς ἴχνεσι τῆς πίστεως NT.Rom.4.12}}; κατ᾽ ἴχνος ᾄσσειν, κατ᾽ ἴχνη διώκειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 32, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 410b, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1059 (Lyric poetry); εἰς ἴχνος τινὸς ἰέναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 330e}}; ἴ. μετιέναι, μετελθεῖν, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phdr.” 276d, “Tht.” 187e}}; ἴχνους προσάπτεσθαι hit upon a **trail**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Plt.” 290d; τοῖς ἀρχαίοις ἴ. ἐς τὰ θεμέλια χρωμένους 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 2.66b; ἴχνη ὑποψίας εἴς τινα φέρει 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.3.10; μήτ᾽ ἴ. μήτ᾽ αἴθυγμα.. παραδιδόντων 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 29, compare “Rh.” 1.91 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}
**2** _poetry_, **foot**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1134, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 7.20.}}
**3** **hard sole of the foot**, {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|LXX.Deu.11.24, +others, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 10.876, 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 47.9.7}}; **sole of a shoe**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 62, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Indica” 16.5}}; **sandal,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|POxy. 1449.51 (_plural_, 3rd c.AD).}}
**4** τὰ ἴ. τῶν χειρῶν the **palms** of the hands, {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.5.4.}}
**5** ἴ. ἀνθρώπινον, as a measure of length, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus “ἀνατομή” 31.}}
**6** **track, route** in the desert, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PRyl.” 197.8 (2nd c.AD).}}
**7** _plural_, **representations of footprints** as votive offerings indicating the presence of a God, ἀνέθηκαν.. κατὰ τὴν τοῦ θεοῦ ἐνέργιαν ἴχνη αὐτοῦ χρύσεα τέσσερα {{Refs|“BCH” 51.106 (from Panamara), etc.}}
